WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.916
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:14.416 --> 00:00:17.410
[BACKGROUND CHATTER]

00:01:19.286 --> 00:01:22.779
ANNOUNCER: Ladies and gentlemen,
please welcome Roger Penrose,

00:01:22.779 --> 00:01:29.266
Susan Sontag, Edward O.
Wilson, and Alan Lightman.

00:01:29.266 --> 00:01:32.260
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:44.234 --> 00:01:45.234
LIGHTMAN: I'll sit here.

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:04.690
Welcome to our
evening discussion

00:02:04.690 --> 00:02:06.910
of image and meaning.

00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:13.060
I'm Alan Lightman of MIT, and
it is my pleasure and privilege

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:15.860
to be the host of
tonight's session.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:22.130
In the history of
modern science,

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:25.140
a central measure
of progress has

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:27.120
been the degree
to which knowledge

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:33.360
can be expressed in numbers
and equations and concepts.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:38.850
A ball takes 1.3 seconds to
fall a distance of 30 feet.

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:41.940
The electrical force
between two electrons

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:45.930
varies as the square of
the distance between them.

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:51.570
The energy of a closed
system is always the same.

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:56.040
To represent a concept
or a result in this form

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:00.540
has usually been considered
the most precise form

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:02.350
of understanding nature.

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:09.080
But knowledge can
also be presented

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:14.570
and misrepresented in
pictures and images.

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:19.100
Our minds and imaginations
react to these representations

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:23.600
in a different way than
to numbers and equations.

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:29.210
And here, we must remember that
we can objectify and distill

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:33.800
all that we want, but
meaning and understanding

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:39.260
must ultimately be
assigned by the human mind.

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:44.060
Image and meaning is really
a matter of human perception.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:52.190
We're sitting here tonight
in one of the world's temples

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:56.510
of science and
technology, dedicated

00:03:56.510 --> 00:04:01.940
to the study of physical
phenomena, atoms and molecules,

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:07.130
DNA, computers, galaxies.

00:04:07.130 --> 00:04:11.040
But of all the wonderful
mysteries of nature,

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:17.940
nothing is more mysterious or
profound than the human mind.

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:23.400
Somehow, we are aware of
ourselves and our relationship

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:26.040
to the outside world.

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:28.890
Somehow, we think.

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:35.190
Somehow, we assign meaning to
the billions of sensory inputs

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:38.310
constantly flooding our brains.

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:43.310
Somehow, we create new ideas.

00:04:43.310 --> 00:04:46.730
More specifically, in the
context of our gathering

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:51.500
tonight and our conference
topic of image and meaning,

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:54.650
I want to raise the
following four questions.

00:04:57.910 --> 00:05:01.020
What role do images
and visualization

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:02.715
play in the creative process?

00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:12.520
How do images change the way
that we think of ourselves?

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:16.450
How do images help
scientists in their research

00:05:16.450 --> 00:05:21.050
beyond the help provided
by numerical data?

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:24.650
And finally, in what
ways do scientists

00:05:24.650 --> 00:05:26.480
communicate their work?

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:29.480
In what ways do images
help scientists communicate

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:34.330
their work to non-scientists?

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:37.990
To stimulate our thought
tonight about these and related

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:43.210
questions, we have with us three
of the most interesting minds

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:45.080
of our time--

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:49.520
Roger Penrose, Susan Sontag,
and Edward O. Wilson.

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:57.640
Roger Penrose is a mathematician
and physicist at Oxford.

00:05:57.640 --> 00:06:01.780
In the 1960s, Penrose proved
very general conditions

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:06.570
under which the universe had to
have originated in a big bang.

00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:11.560
Penrose often uses pictures
and diagrams in his work.

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:15.550
He is renowned for his discovery
of Penrose tiles, which

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:19.090
are two geometric shapes
that can completely

00:06:19.090 --> 00:06:24.670
cover an infinite plane without
ever repeating a pattern.

00:06:24.670 --> 00:06:27.220
In his book The
Emperor's New Mind,

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:30.430
Penrose broadened his
interests to consider

00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:34.420
the nature of thinking
and human consciousness.

00:06:34.420 --> 00:06:37.120
Among his many
awards, Penrose has

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:41.020
won the Wolf Prize, which he
shared with Stephen Hawking

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:43.000
in 1988.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:48.700
And I learned tonight that a
collection of Roger's drawings

00:06:48.700 --> 00:06:52.630
will be shown in the Ruskin
School of Fine Arts at Oxford.

00:06:56.920 --> 00:07:01.870
Susan Sontag is one of the great
literary and cultural voices

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:03.720
of America.

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:06.540
In both fiction
and nonfiction, she

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:10.320
explores topics ranging from
the American consciousness

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:13.890
and identity, to the
cultural and literary meaning

00:07:13.890 --> 00:07:18.300
of illness, to the different
complexions of the narrative

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:21.570
voice in the modern novel.

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:23.790
Her nonfiction
includes such books

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:28.080
as Against Interpretation
and Other Essays,

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:33.000
Illness as Metaphor, and
Under the Sign of Saturn.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:36.750
And her novels include
The Benefactor,

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:43.150
Death Kit, The Volcano Lover,
and, most recently, In America.

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:45.310
Sontag is a winner
of the National Book

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:49.870
Award and the National
Book Critics Circle Award,

00:07:49.870 --> 00:07:53.860
and just recently, she has
won the prestigious Jerusalem

00:07:53.860 --> 00:07:55.540
International Book Award.

00:07:59.290 --> 00:08:03.490
Edward O. Wilson is an
evolutionary biologist

00:08:03.490 --> 00:08:06.580
and zoologist at Harvard.

00:08:06.580 --> 00:08:11.890
Wilson is equally famous for
his decades-long, detailed study

00:08:11.890 --> 00:08:15.700
of the tiny ant and
his broad analyses

00:08:15.700 --> 00:08:18.400
and theories about the
biological principles

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:23.410
that govern social behavior
and organization in all kinds

00:08:23.410 --> 00:08:27.470
of animals, including us.

00:08:27.470 --> 00:08:31.520
Some of Wilson's many books
include The Ants, On Human

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:35.870
Nature, and Sociobiology.

00:08:35.870 --> 00:08:38.870
One of his most recent
books, Consilience,

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:43.650
concerns commonalities between
all forms of human knowledge.

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:46.220
Wilson, among his
other awards, has twice

00:08:46.220 --> 00:08:48.590
won the Pulitzer Prize.

00:08:48.590 --> 00:08:50.420
And I learned from
him tonight that he

00:08:50.420 --> 00:08:58.620
is at work on another book on
what of all subjects but ants.

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:07.320
800 pages, including over 5,000
of his own drawings of ants.

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:10.530
I want to begin by
giving a brief summary

00:09:10.530 --> 00:09:14.000
of the format for the evening.

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:18.080
We will start with a series of
six images, which you will all

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:21.200
see here on the
screen, and I hope

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:24.990
that our panelists can see
here pr by straining your neck

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:27.260
and looking backwards.

00:09:27.260 --> 00:09:29.360
These are images
which have not been

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:32.600
shown before to our guests.

00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:34.670
Each of our guests
will then speak

00:09:34.670 --> 00:09:38.884
for 10 minutes, during
which he or she--

00:09:38.884 --> 00:09:40.712
[LAUGHTER]

00:09:40.712 --> 00:09:43.350
Is something happening?

00:09:43.350 --> 00:09:44.710
OK.

00:09:44.710 --> 00:09:48.640
All right, let me know
when something happens.

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:52.210
Each of our guests will then
speak for about 10 minutes,

00:09:52.210 --> 00:09:56.920
in which he or she can either
give a spontaneous reaction

00:09:56.920 --> 00:10:00.100
to the images, or address
the four questions that I

00:10:00.100 --> 00:10:05.306
brought up earlier, or speak
on any topic that is relevant--

00:10:05.306 --> 00:10:08.144
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:14.040
--to our conference.

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:16.530
Then, we'll have
about 20 minutes

00:10:16.530 --> 00:10:20.550
of dialogue between the
three of them, in which

00:10:20.550 --> 00:10:23.010
they can talk to each other.

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:25.650
I may insert a question or two.

00:10:25.650 --> 00:10:27.690
And after that,
I'll open the floor

00:10:27.690 --> 00:10:32.580
for questions and comments
for another half hour.

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:36.840
So, are you ready
to have some fun?

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:37.350
Good.

00:10:37.350 --> 00:10:39.360
Well, let's begin
with the images.

00:13:00.670 --> 00:13:04.990
Roger, can we start with you?

00:13:04.990 --> 00:13:07.070
PENROSE: OK.

00:13:07.070 --> 00:13:10.850
Well, let me comment
first on your images here.

00:13:10.850 --> 00:13:12.920
I mean, the first
one, presumably,

00:13:12.920 --> 00:13:16.460
is an atomic bomb
explosion, which

00:13:16.460 --> 00:13:23.480
I take to represent the issue of
science and the responsibility

00:13:23.480 --> 00:13:28.670
that one has in developing,
in this case, nuclear energy.

00:13:28.670 --> 00:13:31.040
I'm certainly one of
those people who believes

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:33.059
that you can't really--

00:13:33.059 --> 00:13:34.850
I mean, some people
think that science, OK,

00:13:34.850 --> 00:13:36.290
that's a pure activity.

00:13:36.290 --> 00:13:37.350
You can do it.

00:13:37.350 --> 00:13:40.769
You don't have to worry about
the social consequences.

00:13:40.769 --> 00:13:42.310
I mean, there's
something in me which

00:13:42.310 --> 00:13:44.170
believes that, too, that
science is something that should

00:13:44.170 --> 00:13:45.400
be done for its own sake.

00:13:45.400 --> 00:13:47.440
But on the other hand,
it does seem to me

00:13:47.440 --> 00:13:50.950
that the scientists, after
all, are the people who

00:13:50.950 --> 00:13:56.140
have the best knowledge of
what their discoveries are

00:13:56.140 --> 00:13:56.910
likely to do.

00:13:56.910 --> 00:13:59.260
And so, therefore, they
do have a responsibility

00:13:59.260 --> 00:14:02.170
to at least pay attention
to these issues.

00:14:02.170 --> 00:14:03.670
So I'm certainly
one of these people

00:14:03.670 --> 00:14:06.250
who believes that
you can't really

00:14:06.250 --> 00:14:10.540
separate the pure science from
what potential applications

00:14:10.540 --> 00:14:13.450
it might have and whether
they'll be useful for benefits

00:14:13.450 --> 00:14:15.250
or otherwise.

00:14:15.250 --> 00:14:16.930
Of course, scientists
are not always

00:14:16.930 --> 00:14:18.650
very accurate in their
predictions in this way.

00:14:18.650 --> 00:14:20.899
And I think there's some
famous quote from Rutherford,

00:14:20.899 --> 00:14:22.720
who thought that nuclear
energy would never

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:24.110
be useful for anything.

00:14:24.110 --> 00:14:26.074
So you can't always
believe what they say.

00:14:26.074 --> 00:14:28.240
But on the other hand, they
are in a better position

00:14:28.240 --> 00:14:29.480
than other people.

00:14:29.480 --> 00:14:31.960
So the responsibility is there.

00:14:31.960 --> 00:14:35.120
I take the second image to
be representative-- well,

00:14:35.120 --> 00:14:38.680
it certainly is the Earth
rising from the moon.

00:14:38.680 --> 00:14:41.370
And it does represent a
fantastic achievement,

00:14:41.370 --> 00:14:43.817
a technological achievement.

00:14:43.817 --> 00:14:45.400
Not so much as
scientific achievement,

00:14:45.400 --> 00:14:46.858
although there is,
of course, that.

00:14:46.858 --> 00:14:48.420
It goes back to Newton.

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:52.660
One appreciates
that space travel

00:14:52.660 --> 00:14:54.380
was potentially possible.

00:14:54.380 --> 00:14:57.070
But it's really a
technological achievement,

00:14:57.070 --> 00:15:00.500
which required a great
deal of money and so on.

00:15:00.500 --> 00:15:02.320
And I feel a great
thrill in this.

00:15:02.320 --> 00:15:05.410
I was one of these people
who stayed up all night

00:15:05.410 --> 00:15:08.110
when the first moon
landings came out and so on.

00:15:08.110 --> 00:15:11.240
I think it's a great thing
that people can do this.

00:15:11.240 --> 00:15:12.740
Of course, they're
not doing it now,

00:15:12.740 --> 00:15:15.730
but maybe they'll
go to Mars one day.

00:15:15.730 --> 00:15:17.800
And so I like that
kind of thing.

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:18.950
I think it's great.

00:15:18.950 --> 00:15:21.070
But it is expensive,
of course, and one

00:15:21.070 --> 00:15:25.210
has to worry about how these
things affect other research

00:15:25.210 --> 00:15:27.100
which might be going on.

00:15:27.100 --> 00:15:29.860
Here we have Crick and
Watson, and of course, this

00:15:29.860 --> 00:15:32.830
was a fantastic thing
they did to discover

00:15:32.830 --> 00:15:41.470
the structure of DNA, and it did
set off a tremendous revolution

00:15:41.470 --> 00:15:42.750
in biology.

00:15:42.750 --> 00:15:44.750
It probably represents a
number of other things,

00:15:44.750 --> 00:15:48.840
because in a sense,
I seem to remember

00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:51.300
from reading Watson's
book, or somewhere,

00:15:51.300 --> 00:15:53.531
that they argued
how ignorant they

00:15:53.531 --> 00:15:56.030
were of some of these things
at the time they were doing it.

00:15:56.030 --> 00:15:58.524
And maybe it's a case
for not knowing too much.

00:15:58.524 --> 00:15:59.940
Because if you
don't know too much

00:15:59.940 --> 00:16:01.410
about what other
people are doing,

00:16:01.410 --> 00:16:04.100
you can bring a new
perspective on a subject.

00:16:04.100 --> 00:16:07.080
And maybe that's an
illustration of that.

00:16:07.080 --> 00:16:09.944
It certainly was a wonderful
scientific achievement.

00:16:09.944 --> 00:16:11.235
There's no question about that.

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:16.840
And we're going to see
further developments

00:16:16.840 --> 00:16:20.500
from that in the Genome
Project and so on.

00:16:20.500 --> 00:16:22.270
And then, again,
one has these issues

00:16:22.270 --> 00:16:25.810
of social consequences, which
maybe will come up again

00:16:25.810 --> 00:16:28.220
in one of the later images.

00:16:28.220 --> 00:16:34.510
The development from-- well,
that's supposed to be a great--

00:16:34.510 --> 00:16:36.820
showing how human
beings are somehow

00:16:36.820 --> 00:16:38.200
the pinnacle of evolution.

00:16:41.740 --> 00:16:43.841
Of course, there
are cartoons which

00:16:43.841 --> 00:16:46.090
show these things going
opposite directions and so on,

00:16:46.090 --> 00:16:49.060
and they start coming
back down again.

00:16:49.060 --> 00:16:51.480
It's a wonderful image.

00:16:51.480 --> 00:16:53.590
It may not be--

00:16:53.590 --> 00:16:56.850
well, it also reflects,
somehow, that human beings are

00:16:56.850 --> 00:17:01.050
part of animal life as
a whole and that there

00:17:01.050 --> 00:17:05.130
isn't a dividing line between
the humans and other animals,

00:17:05.130 --> 00:17:06.869
which I believe very strongly.

00:17:06.869 --> 00:17:10.920
So that when one talks about
things like the human mind,

00:17:10.920 --> 00:17:12.720
one is really
talking about mind,

00:17:12.720 --> 00:17:14.730
and mind is not just human mind.

00:17:14.730 --> 00:17:16.901
One has to think about animals.

00:17:16.901 --> 00:17:18.359
It's something that
I worried about

00:17:18.359 --> 00:17:22.270
with all this great business
with foot-and-mouth in the UK

00:17:22.270 --> 00:17:26.730
and with all this tremendous
slaughter of cattle and so on.

00:17:26.730 --> 00:17:29.640
And nobody ever mentioned
that, somehow, there were

00:17:29.640 --> 00:17:30.771
any rights for the animals.

00:17:30.771 --> 00:17:33.270
I mean, it was all to do with
what was economically the best

00:17:33.270 --> 00:17:34.884
thing and so on and so forth.

00:17:34.884 --> 00:17:36.300
And I didn't hear
a single person,

00:17:36.300 --> 00:17:38.820
certainly not a politician,
mention that should we

00:17:38.820 --> 00:17:42.840
think whether an infinite
number of animals

00:17:42.840 --> 00:17:44.710
is equivalent to one
human or something.

00:17:44.710 --> 00:17:46.110
Is that a fair equation?

00:17:46.110 --> 00:17:48.480
It seemed to me that it goes
against the grain very much

00:17:48.480 --> 00:17:50.370
with me.

00:17:50.370 --> 00:17:53.890
Here, it looks like
a fetus, or maybe a

00:17:53.890 --> 00:17:56.730
call it a baby at this
stage, in the womb, which

00:17:56.730 --> 00:18:02.490
does raise certain issues
of the relation of science

00:18:02.490 --> 00:18:03.960
to moral issues.

00:18:06.790 --> 00:18:10.600
More or less, when does
life begin, and so on?

00:18:10.600 --> 00:18:13.070
Well, I think it's a
difficult question.

00:18:13.070 --> 00:18:18.310
I certainly don't believe
that one should regard

00:18:18.310 --> 00:18:21.460
a few-week-old fetus as having--

00:18:21.460 --> 00:18:23.740
I mean, think, there are
issues we don't know yet.

00:18:23.740 --> 00:18:26.080
And this probably relates
to, perhaps, the next image,

00:18:26.080 --> 00:18:28.900
too, which is the
question of a brain

00:18:28.900 --> 00:18:30.620
and what's going
on in the brain.

00:18:30.620 --> 00:18:34.915
And here we have imaging
of there is presumably

00:18:34.915 --> 00:18:36.540
somebody thinking
about various things,

00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:39.680
and where is it to be
pinpointed in the brain?

00:18:39.680 --> 00:18:41.560
Of course, that doesn't
tell us a great deal

00:18:41.560 --> 00:18:44.770
about what it is to have
an awareness of something.

00:18:44.770 --> 00:18:47.470
So although it's a tremendous
achievement in science

00:18:47.470 --> 00:18:51.490
to find out what goes on
where in different parts

00:18:51.490 --> 00:18:53.350
of the brain, does
it really tell us

00:18:53.350 --> 00:18:56.770
what it is that's going on when
somebody is aware of something,

00:18:56.770 --> 00:18:58.120
of consciousness?

00:18:58.120 --> 00:19:02.890
So I think there's a
great deal that science

00:19:02.890 --> 00:19:07.060
is going to have to do before
we answer this question.

00:19:07.060 --> 00:19:08.726
I'm certainly one
of these people--

00:19:08.726 --> 00:19:10.350
I've written about
these things and get

00:19:10.350 --> 00:19:13.360
into lots of trouble
with people sometimes.

00:19:13.360 --> 00:19:15.820
But my general view
is that we simply

00:19:15.820 --> 00:19:19.240
don't know yet what's
really going on, even

00:19:19.240 --> 00:19:20.080
in a serious way.

00:19:20.080 --> 00:19:22.180
We're going to have to
know more physics than we

00:19:22.180 --> 00:19:25.060
do at the moment before we can
know what could be going on,

00:19:25.060 --> 00:19:27.440
even, to evoke consciousness.

00:19:27.440 --> 00:19:29.350
So the issue of
consciousness, then,

00:19:29.350 --> 00:19:32.610
relates to the central, the
red one in the middle there.

00:19:32.610 --> 00:19:36.880
And it's a question of when
does the fetus actually become

00:19:36.880 --> 00:19:39.220
a person in the sense
of being conscious.

00:19:39.220 --> 00:19:40.670
My guess is it's fairly early.

00:19:40.670 --> 00:19:42.802
But I don't think it's
a matter of a few weeks.

00:19:42.802 --> 00:19:45.010
I think you have to have a
significant looking thing,

00:19:45.010 --> 00:19:47.050
but that's a guess.

00:19:47.050 --> 00:19:49.700
The question is, we really
have to know these things.

00:19:49.700 --> 00:19:53.764
So I would think a
baby in the womb,

00:19:53.764 --> 00:19:55.180
if you could still
call it a baby,

00:19:55.180 --> 00:19:59.650
then certainly it does have
some kind of consciousness.

00:19:59.650 --> 00:20:01.840
I would guess this
from experiences

00:20:01.840 --> 00:20:03.760
that I've had recently,
because my wife is--

00:20:03.760 --> 00:20:05.690
I have a one-year-old child now.

00:20:05.690 --> 00:20:12.940
And I remember the baby was
sensitive to music already,

00:20:12.940 --> 00:20:15.016
I would say, about a
month before it was born.

00:20:15.016 --> 00:20:16.390
So I think there
are things going

00:20:16.390 --> 00:20:18.650
on there at that late stage.

00:20:18.650 --> 00:20:22.380
But these are questions
which, at the moment,

00:20:22.380 --> 00:20:24.260
we have no handle on.

00:20:24.260 --> 00:20:26.330
And we're going to
have to understand

00:20:26.330 --> 00:20:29.720
a lot more about what
consciousness is about

00:20:29.720 --> 00:20:33.180
before we can really have
a handle on these issues

00:20:33.180 --> 00:20:38.800
of the moral questions in
relation to unborn children

00:20:38.800 --> 00:20:40.050
and so on.

00:20:40.050 --> 00:20:44.104
Well, let's see, how many more
minutes do I have, if any?

00:20:44.104 --> 00:20:44.770
LIGHTMAN: Three.

00:20:44.770 --> 00:20:46.950
PENROSE: Three, OK.

00:20:46.950 --> 00:20:49.380
Let me make a few comments
about some of the things

00:20:49.380 --> 00:20:52.530
I've been seeing in the
conference up to this point.

00:20:52.530 --> 00:20:54.720
I must say, I've found
it quite fascinating,

00:20:54.720 --> 00:20:58.650
and I do think that there
are tremendous developments.

00:20:58.650 --> 00:21:00.630
A lot of these have
to do with computers,

00:21:00.630 --> 00:21:03.720
and others have to do
with ways in which people

00:21:03.720 --> 00:21:07.320
can explore very small
objects and so on and very

00:21:07.320 --> 00:21:11.240
distant objects and so on.

00:21:11.240 --> 00:21:15.200
I did find the things
about the films last night

00:21:15.200 --> 00:21:17.840
very moving, in some
ways, and very impressive.

00:21:17.840 --> 00:21:20.022
But there are moral issues
which come up there.

00:21:20.022 --> 00:21:21.980
And I think one has to
face up to these things.

00:21:21.980 --> 00:21:22.880
One of these things,
I think, that

00:21:22.880 --> 00:21:24.838
was today maybe about
the question of doctoring

00:21:24.838 --> 00:21:26.550
photographs and so on.

00:21:26.550 --> 00:21:29.900
I just wonder whether
there oughtn't

00:21:29.900 --> 00:21:33.050
to be some sort of a law
which says that you can't

00:21:33.050 --> 00:21:37.810
use a photograph and doctor it
without saying that you've done

00:21:37.810 --> 00:21:38.930
and what you've done.

00:21:38.930 --> 00:21:42.140
So it seems to me using
a photograph is fine,

00:21:42.140 --> 00:21:46.010
but if it has been changed
in some way, that is illegal

00:21:46.010 --> 00:21:48.350
unless you actually say
the change has been made.

00:21:48.350 --> 00:21:50.180
And it seems to me,
I would very much

00:21:50.180 --> 00:21:53.092
support some sort of
law of that nature.

00:21:53.092 --> 00:21:54.800
It was just something
that occurred to me

00:21:54.800 --> 00:21:56.091
when I was seeing these things.

00:21:56.091 --> 00:21:58.329
Let me make one
slightly trivial point.

00:21:58.329 --> 00:21:59.870
Frivolous, I was
going to say, point.

00:21:59.870 --> 00:22:01.094
It's not frivolous, actually.

00:22:01.094 --> 00:22:02.510
But one thing,
also, I was feeling

00:22:02.510 --> 00:22:05.390
when I was noticing people
giving their talks here

00:22:05.390 --> 00:22:07.250
was that there's some
respects in which

00:22:07.250 --> 00:22:11.540
the developments in technology
are not really in advance.

00:22:11.540 --> 00:22:14.466
And one of these, one of my
little bugbears, I'm afraid,

00:22:14.466 --> 00:22:17.090
I've noticed that when I sit at
the back of a room and somebody

00:22:17.090 --> 00:22:20.420
points at something with
this wonderful laser pointer,

00:22:20.420 --> 00:22:21.630
I simply can't see a thing.

00:22:21.630 --> 00:22:23.396
He says, this thing over here.

00:22:23.396 --> 00:22:24.190
What?

00:22:24.190 --> 00:22:26.725
And maybe by the time--

00:22:26.725 --> 00:22:28.850
if he's held it on the same
point for five minutes,

00:22:28.850 --> 00:22:29.960
maybe I would see it.

00:22:29.960 --> 00:22:33.080
But a good old stick we used
to have pointing at something,

00:22:33.080 --> 00:22:35.180
yeah, that's fine.

00:22:35.180 --> 00:22:37.230
LIGHTMAN: One minute.

00:22:37.230 --> 00:22:39.230
PENROSE: It just seems
to me that sometimes

00:22:39.230 --> 00:22:43.200
technology, just by itself,
is not necessarily an advance.

00:22:43.200 --> 00:22:45.936
Other times, it is.

00:22:45.936 --> 00:22:49.220
One can't tell,
but one certainly

00:22:49.220 --> 00:22:52.140
has to take on what
the advantages are.

00:22:52.140 --> 00:22:53.570
But not just because it's new.

00:22:53.570 --> 00:22:55.070
I don't think one
should necessarily

00:22:55.070 --> 00:22:56.530
be driven in that direction.

00:22:56.530 --> 00:22:58.870
Were there any other
points I should make?

00:22:58.870 --> 00:22:59.600
Yes.

00:22:59.600 --> 00:23:01.110
I guess I should
make this point,

00:23:01.110 --> 00:23:05.101
that, I mean, visual
images I don't regard

00:23:05.101 --> 00:23:06.600
as something
fundamentally different

00:23:06.600 --> 00:23:08.250
from other means
of communication.

00:23:08.250 --> 00:23:11.650
It's just that it's a way in
which you convey understanding,

00:23:11.650 --> 00:23:14.337
often, very rapidly,
which depends

00:23:14.337 --> 00:23:15.420
on the subject, of course.

00:23:15.420 --> 00:23:16.670
But sometimes you can do this.

00:23:16.670 --> 00:23:19.750
Sometimes, words, you
need a lot more of them.

00:23:19.750 --> 00:23:21.990
Sometimes the image is
pretty incomprehensible.

00:23:21.990 --> 00:23:23.410
You need to describe it.

00:23:23.410 --> 00:23:25.390
But it is a very powerful thing.

00:23:25.390 --> 00:23:27.720
And I think with technology
that's been coming up,

00:23:27.720 --> 00:23:32.430
it's something which will
increase in its importance.

00:23:32.430 --> 00:23:36.670
And I like that, because
I like visual images.

00:23:36.670 --> 00:23:39.120
But as technology
develops, there

00:23:39.120 --> 00:23:41.640
is a slightly negative
element, which perhaps I

00:23:41.640 --> 00:23:45.360
would like to bring up, which
is the ease of communication

00:23:45.360 --> 00:23:47.010
isn't always a positive thing.

00:23:47.010 --> 00:23:50.340
Because it does mean
that fashionable ideas

00:23:50.340 --> 00:23:52.305
spread the globe
almost instantaneously.

00:23:52.305 --> 00:23:53.930
Whereas, in the good,
old days-- here's

00:23:53.930 --> 00:23:55.846
where I want to go back
to some things which I

00:23:55.846 --> 00:23:57.372
like about the good, old days.

00:23:57.372 --> 00:23:59.205
You had these pockets
of people working away

00:23:59.205 --> 00:24:03.930
at different things, and
there was a kind of--

00:24:03.930 --> 00:24:06.454
people didn't have to do what
everybody else was doing.

00:24:06.454 --> 00:24:08.370
Whereas, with the
internet-- internet is fine.

00:24:08.370 --> 00:24:12.470
And it's great that people
in, say, developing countries

00:24:12.470 --> 00:24:16.207
can see what's going on in
other countries and so on

00:24:16.207 --> 00:24:18.540
without having to buy the
journals and things like that.

00:24:18.540 --> 00:24:19.770
OK.

00:24:19.770 --> 00:24:22.169
And international cooperation
and so on is great.

00:24:22.169 --> 00:24:24.210
But on the other hand,
there is a downside, which

00:24:24.210 --> 00:24:27.420
means that fashionable
ideas sometimes

00:24:27.420 --> 00:24:30.720
have a much greater hold,
I think, in modern science

00:24:30.720 --> 00:24:33.810
than they did before
communication was so easy.

00:24:33.810 --> 00:24:36.630
So I think that's something that
one has to face up to and maybe

00:24:36.630 --> 00:24:39.900
see how one could move
things in a way which

00:24:39.900 --> 00:24:44.730
increases the possibility of
having more variety in what

00:24:44.730 --> 00:24:45.320
they do.

00:24:45.320 --> 00:24:46.620
So I'd just make that comment.

00:24:46.620 --> 00:24:47.740
LIGHTMAN: Thank you.

00:24:47.740 --> 00:24:51.470
We'll let Susan speak now.

00:24:51.470 --> 00:24:57.740
SONTAG: Well, there are a very
large and wonderful subjects

00:24:57.740 --> 00:25:03.300
on the table, such as the
role of visual information

00:25:03.300 --> 00:25:09.160
or visualizing in
the creative process

00:25:09.160 --> 00:25:13.730
and what it means to
represent knowledge visually,

00:25:13.730 --> 00:25:17.780
which I presume in
the word represent

00:25:17.780 --> 00:25:21.830
is entailed, also, the notion
of communicating knowledge

00:25:21.830 --> 00:25:28.570
and how visualizing and
visual representation

00:25:28.570 --> 00:25:30.680
aids in the pedagogic
process, for instance.

00:25:30.680 --> 00:25:32.570
Those are very important issues.

00:25:32.570 --> 00:25:35.180
But I think I will
wait for a moment

00:25:35.180 --> 00:25:39.920
and just address these
fascinating images.

00:25:39.920 --> 00:25:44.420
I guess the first
thing I would say

00:25:44.420 --> 00:25:48.670
is that what strikes
me about these images,

00:25:48.670 --> 00:25:54.110
and Roger Penrose,
in a way, responded

00:25:54.110 --> 00:25:57.350
to what I think is at the heart
of this particular display

00:25:57.350 --> 00:26:02.610
of six images, is he took
them as representative.

00:26:02.610 --> 00:26:05.600
He was invited, we're all
invited, to respond to them.

00:26:05.600 --> 00:26:08.570
But to say that
they're representative

00:26:08.570 --> 00:26:10.850
is not the only way
of responding to them.

00:26:10.850 --> 00:26:14.620
But what he did
was to say, well,

00:26:14.620 --> 00:26:18.450
that image of the atom
bomb or the hydrogen bomb,

00:26:18.450 --> 00:26:21.620
whatever it is, reminds
us of the ethical issues

00:26:21.620 --> 00:26:25.820
in certain kinds of scientific
and technological work.

00:26:25.820 --> 00:26:29.420
The image of the Earth
seen from the moon

00:26:29.420 --> 00:26:35.200
reminds us or suggests
questions about space travel.

00:26:35.200 --> 00:26:38.150
Crick and Watson,
let's say, remind us

00:26:38.150 --> 00:26:42.120
of the glories of
scientific achievement,

00:26:42.120 --> 00:26:43.110
et cetera, et cetera.

00:26:43.110 --> 00:26:45.980
Each of them are taken
as representative

00:26:45.980 --> 00:26:50.790
of a thought, a problem,
a task, a confluence.

00:26:54.140 --> 00:26:59.360
I'm rather suspicious of this
way of thinking about images.

00:26:59.360 --> 00:27:04.130
I think it's more
and more foisted

00:27:04.130 --> 00:27:08.930
on us by our televisual
culture, but I

00:27:08.930 --> 00:27:14.000
think it's actually a
rather shallow approach

00:27:14.000 --> 00:27:16.160
to thinking about images.

00:27:16.160 --> 00:27:18.020
I'm not saying you are shallow.

00:27:18.020 --> 00:27:21.470
I'm saying that this
display encourages--

00:27:21.470 --> 00:27:22.640
please understand me.

00:27:22.640 --> 00:27:25.670
This display
precisely encourages

00:27:25.670 --> 00:27:27.302
that way of looking at images.

00:27:27.302 --> 00:27:28.760
I'm not talking
about the response,

00:27:28.760 --> 00:27:31.160
because I think it's so
it's a very normal response.

00:27:31.160 --> 00:27:35.570
But you put images which are,
in fact, famous images-- no,

00:27:35.570 --> 00:27:37.280
please, bear with me.

00:27:37.280 --> 00:27:38.870
These are famous images.

00:27:38.870 --> 00:27:41.060
These are celebrity images.

00:27:41.060 --> 00:27:44.900
These are images
chosen for the fact

00:27:44.900 --> 00:27:48.860
that even I, a
scientific illiterate,

00:27:48.860 --> 00:27:51.260
know what all of
those images are.

00:27:51.260 --> 00:27:54.330
I recognize them.

00:27:54.330 --> 00:27:58.580
Very few people in this space
are as ignorant of science

00:27:58.580 --> 00:28:02.000
as I am, but I recognize
all six of these images.

00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:05.210
I know exactly what
is being shown there.

00:28:05.210 --> 00:28:08.720
So they are chosen precisely
because they're representative,

00:28:08.720 --> 00:28:12.710
precisely because they are,
as it were, celebrity images.

00:28:12.710 --> 00:28:15.500
Or let me put it
even more bluntly.

00:28:15.500 --> 00:28:19.550
They are the visual
equivalent of sound bites.

00:28:19.550 --> 00:28:23.900
I think this is
not a very good way

00:28:23.900 --> 00:28:25.910
into the question of images.

00:28:25.910 --> 00:28:32.870
I think it encourages
thinking about images

00:28:32.870 --> 00:28:36.410
in a way that takes us
away from real knowledge.

00:28:36.410 --> 00:28:40.670
I mean, there is, after all, I
think an argument for saying--

00:28:40.670 --> 00:28:42.840
a case, rather, to be
made out for the argument

00:28:42.840 --> 00:28:45.380
that we really don't understand
very much through images.

00:28:45.380 --> 00:28:48.740
Images are, at best, only
aids, and they always

00:28:48.740 --> 00:28:50.150
are in a particular context.

00:28:50.150 --> 00:28:52.940
The context of these
images is that we're

00:28:52.940 --> 00:28:55.130
having a conversation,
and this is

00:28:55.130 --> 00:28:56.960
a way of introducing
the conversation.

00:28:56.960 --> 00:29:01.730
But my first reaction was,
oh, those famous images.

00:29:01.730 --> 00:29:03.980
And as Alan Lightman
told you, we

00:29:03.980 --> 00:29:06.050
weren't told what the
images were going to be,

00:29:06.050 --> 00:29:07.680
and they were kind
of a surprise.

00:29:07.680 --> 00:29:10.280
And when they came up,
I thought, oh, shit,

00:29:10.280 --> 00:29:11.150
those images.

00:29:11.150 --> 00:29:12.200
I know those images.

00:29:12.200 --> 00:29:17.150
I thought they were going to be
more interesting, more weird.

00:29:17.150 --> 00:29:19.580
The only one that seems
genuinely weird is

00:29:19.580 --> 00:29:21.410
the so-called fetus,
which could be--

00:29:21.410 --> 00:29:23.540
maybe I'm too much
of a movie goer--

00:29:23.540 --> 00:29:26.600
the Starchild in 2001.

00:29:26.600 --> 00:29:29.749
I'm not 100% convinced--
although if Alan tells me

00:29:29.749 --> 00:29:31.040
it is-- that it's a real fetus.

00:29:31.040 --> 00:29:33.620
But anyway, Stanley Kubrick
and his art director

00:29:33.620 --> 00:29:37.060
Douglas Trumbull made
an imitation of that,

00:29:37.060 --> 00:29:39.680
if those of you know that
great movie of Stanley Kubrick.

00:29:39.680 --> 00:29:43.260
It ends with, indeed, a
version of that image.

00:29:43.260 --> 00:29:46.500
So we're not only in
an era of celebrity

00:29:46.500 --> 00:29:50.590
and an era of televisual
reality, which

00:29:50.590 --> 00:29:54.510
is so compelling for people,
we're in an era of celebrity

00:29:54.510 --> 00:29:57.060
images, in which
certain images, then,

00:29:57.060 --> 00:29:58.870
are taken as representative.

00:29:58.870 --> 00:30:02.220
They trigger responses
where we say, OK, this

00:30:02.220 --> 00:30:03.540
is a subject of debate.

00:30:03.540 --> 00:30:05.100
And everything
that Roger Penrose

00:30:05.100 --> 00:30:09.270
said about how we could think
about these problems-- they

00:30:09.270 --> 00:30:11.040
are real problems.

00:30:11.040 --> 00:30:14.520
What I'm questioning
is, more and more,

00:30:14.520 --> 00:30:17.670
a kind of short
circuiting of the long,

00:30:17.670 --> 00:30:21.060
laborious process
that is conducted

00:30:21.060 --> 00:30:23.160
in words and in argument.

00:30:23.160 --> 00:30:25.110
As if, really, we understand--

00:30:25.110 --> 00:30:28.890
I think what's suggested
is that we understand more

00:30:28.890 --> 00:30:31.530
from images than we really do.

00:30:31.530 --> 00:30:34.620
My own view is--

00:30:34.620 --> 00:30:40.290
except, again, thinking
of these 5,000 images

00:30:40.290 --> 00:30:43.860
of ants in 800 pages of text.

00:30:43.860 --> 00:30:46.050
I'm sure these are
incredibly informative,

00:30:46.050 --> 00:30:47.340
but they are in a context.

00:30:47.340 --> 00:30:51.150
I think images, obviously,
always appear in a context.

00:30:51.150 --> 00:30:56.130
And the way we think about
images is contextually driven.

00:30:56.130 --> 00:30:58.050
So I suppose the first
thing I want to say,

00:30:58.050 --> 00:31:00.960
as perverse as it may
seem, is that the context

00:31:00.960 --> 00:31:03.630
of these images is
this discussion.

00:31:03.630 --> 00:31:05.540
And it's completely
adventitious.

00:31:05.540 --> 00:31:08.260
It's, in a way,
completely false.

00:31:08.260 --> 00:31:11.460
In fact, these images have
nothing to do with each other.

00:31:11.460 --> 00:31:16.110
And this use of images,
I think, tends--

00:31:16.110 --> 00:31:19.590
images in series, famous
images, celebrity images.

00:31:19.590 --> 00:31:24.550
In how many contexts--
magazines, books, television,

00:31:24.550 --> 00:31:25.140
et cetera--

00:31:25.140 --> 00:31:27.919
are we invited to
recognize the image?

00:31:27.919 --> 00:31:29.460
In fact, if you go
to a movie theater

00:31:29.460 --> 00:31:33.180
now, after you start
munching on the popcorn

00:31:33.180 --> 00:31:36.240
and before the ads and
the trailers start,

00:31:36.240 --> 00:31:37.710
you may get a lot of images.

00:31:37.710 --> 00:31:40.050
And then you're
invited to recognize

00:31:40.050 --> 00:31:47.340
what movie this is from or
what actor is represented.

00:31:47.340 --> 00:31:52.530
So I'm a little worried about
the extent to which we take

00:31:52.530 --> 00:31:55.050
images as telling us very much.

00:31:55.050 --> 00:31:59.250
My feeling is more we
remember through images,

00:31:59.250 --> 00:32:02.250
but we understand through words.

00:32:02.250 --> 00:32:07.530
And I'm not sure how much we
understand outside of context

00:32:07.530 --> 00:32:10.320
supplied by
preexistent assumptions

00:32:10.320 --> 00:32:14.430
by argument that can
be expressed in words.

00:32:14.430 --> 00:32:17.580
I don't think the images are
telling us really anything

00:32:17.580 --> 00:32:18.270
at all.

00:32:18.270 --> 00:32:22.400
They are very often a
form of entertainment.

00:32:22.400 --> 00:32:24.690
Now, they are
entertaining, and even more

00:32:24.690 --> 00:32:26.520
than that, they give pleasure.

00:32:26.520 --> 00:32:27.930
Images give pleasure.

00:32:27.930 --> 00:32:30.840
They don't only
give information.

00:32:30.840 --> 00:32:32.460
They give pleasure.

00:32:32.460 --> 00:32:38.580
There's also another thing
to be said, obviously.

00:32:38.580 --> 00:32:42.270
It's so obvious that you feel
you don't have to say it,

00:32:42.270 --> 00:32:43.800
and yet, I think
it is worth saying.

00:32:43.800 --> 00:32:46.980
These are all photographs,
and obviously, that is not

00:32:46.980 --> 00:32:49.860
the only form of image making.

00:32:49.860 --> 00:32:52.680
And far from the only
form of image making

00:32:52.680 --> 00:32:56.460
which is instructive
or useful, I

00:32:56.460 --> 00:33:00.360
should imagine, in
scientific work.

00:33:00.360 --> 00:33:04.710
So I'm not so sure what
images are standing

00:33:04.710 --> 00:33:07.500
for except as this
kind of shorthand,

00:33:07.500 --> 00:33:11.760
or what I call the
visual sound bite.

00:33:11.760 --> 00:33:14.520
Yes, it's true, and if
it's a famous image,

00:33:14.520 --> 00:33:16.290
we'll have some
associations with it,

00:33:16.290 --> 00:33:19.540
and we'll have associations
about particular problems.

00:33:19.540 --> 00:33:22.420
But I don't think
we know very much.

00:33:22.420 --> 00:33:24.900
And I don't think,
except insofar

00:33:24.900 --> 00:33:29.790
as any image can be an object
of reverie, an incitement

00:33:29.790 --> 00:33:35.520
to dream, to fantasize,
that the image

00:33:35.520 --> 00:33:37.820
is telling us anything at all.

00:33:37.820 --> 00:33:44.880
And also, images identify
things that we think

00:33:44.880 --> 00:33:46.370
we should be thinking about.

00:33:46.370 --> 00:33:50.730
I've been I've been
thinking a lot about the use

00:33:50.730 --> 00:33:55.710
of photography in alerting us
to catastrophe and disaster

00:33:55.710 --> 00:34:00.339
and giving us a sense of
war and what goes on in war.

00:34:00.339 --> 00:34:02.130
And there's, of course,
a very long history

00:34:02.130 --> 00:34:06.210
of this that goes back, in
photography, to, let's say,

00:34:06.210 --> 00:34:09.150
the Crimean War, which
is the first war that

00:34:09.150 --> 00:34:13.860
had any real coverage
by photography.

00:34:13.860 --> 00:34:17.100
And what's interesting is
what wars we know about

00:34:17.100 --> 00:34:19.290
through photography
and what wars

00:34:19.290 --> 00:34:21.179
we are much less aware
of because we don't

00:34:21.179 --> 00:34:24.360
have photographic
evidence of them,

00:34:24.360 --> 00:34:26.850
photographic witness of them.

00:34:26.850 --> 00:34:32.070
So photographic knowledge
identifies, defines,

00:34:32.070 --> 00:34:36.989
makes memorable, and it also
excludes lots of other things

00:34:36.989 --> 00:34:39.330
that we should-- it not
only points us to things

00:34:39.330 --> 00:34:40.980
we should be thinking about.

00:34:40.980 --> 00:34:43.949
In fact, the repertoire
of famous images

00:34:43.949 --> 00:34:48.270
obscures, occludes, and
hides, I think, at least

00:34:48.270 --> 00:34:51.210
as many, probably more,
issues than the ones

00:34:51.210 --> 00:34:53.940
that it draws to our attention.

00:34:53.940 --> 00:34:56.340
LIGHTMAN: Well, thank you.

00:34:56.340 --> 00:34:59.220
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:02.580 --> 00:35:04.500
Ed?

00:35:04.500 --> 00:35:08.180
WILSON: Well, now, ants having
crawled into the discussion,

00:35:08.180 --> 00:35:12.120
I would like to use
them to first show

00:35:12.120 --> 00:35:15.340
the use, at least in
science, of images

00:35:15.340 --> 00:35:21.970
in the context of creative
work and basic research.

00:35:21.970 --> 00:35:27.100
It's true that I am just
completing the monograph

00:35:27.100 --> 00:35:32.260
of about 625 species of ants.

00:35:32.260 --> 00:35:35.410
Some 335 of them
are new to science,

00:35:35.410 --> 00:35:40.240
and they constitute about 20%
of all the known ant species

00:35:40.240 --> 00:35:41.440
in the Western hemisphere.

00:35:41.440 --> 00:35:44.440
Why am I doing that?

00:35:44.440 --> 00:35:45.445
Well, let me explain.

00:35:48.070 --> 00:35:52.960
And then I have personally
drawn the aspects

00:35:52.960 --> 00:35:56.070
of their anatomy, appearance,
and diagnostic traits

00:35:56.070 --> 00:35:59.740
with over 5,000 drawings.

00:35:59.740 --> 00:36:04.600
This is part of the process of
addressing a group of organisms

00:36:04.600 --> 00:36:10.690
that you love, that you
really care about and are

00:36:10.690 --> 00:36:11.940
intellectually excited about.

00:36:11.940 --> 00:36:13.740
But it's much more than that.

00:36:13.740 --> 00:36:17.050
As you go into a
monograph of this kind,

00:36:17.050 --> 00:36:21.340
you are living
within the subject.

00:36:21.340 --> 00:36:24.580
You're looking at
all of the aspects

00:36:24.580 --> 00:36:26.830
of the traits of the species.

00:36:26.830 --> 00:36:28.360
You are drawing them yourself.

00:36:28.360 --> 00:36:32.890
You're getting that
visual feedback.

00:36:32.890 --> 00:36:38.140
In biology, especially,
we are focused on

00:36:38.140 --> 00:36:40.400
and we are driven by images.

00:36:40.400 --> 00:36:44.502
So we learn in detail,
and it goes deep

00:36:44.502 --> 00:36:45.460
into our consciousness.

00:36:45.460 --> 00:36:48.580
And then, part of this is
collecting these creatures

00:36:48.580 --> 00:36:53.270
in the field, of getting the
sense of where they live,

00:36:53.270 --> 00:36:56.760
entering the actual
habitats and looking

00:36:56.760 --> 00:36:58.660
at their ecology
and their behavior.

00:36:58.660 --> 00:37:00.400
You learn more and more.

00:37:00.400 --> 00:37:04.420
And this, then,
helps us illustrate

00:37:04.420 --> 00:37:08.470
what I perceive as the two
strategies of doing research

00:37:08.470 --> 00:37:11.020
in biology.

00:37:11.020 --> 00:37:15.010
First is-- well, lets call
it two types of biologists,

00:37:15.010 --> 00:37:17.350
to be a stereotypical about it.

00:37:17.350 --> 00:37:21.190
The first type
addresses a problem

00:37:21.190 --> 00:37:25.030
and knows that for every
problem in biology,

00:37:25.030 --> 00:37:29.510
there is an organism ideally
suited to its solution.

00:37:29.510 --> 00:37:32.740
So that's bacteria
for molecular genetic.

00:37:32.740 --> 00:37:36.550
The other loves the
organism, wishes

00:37:36.550 --> 00:37:39.850
to find out everything
he can about it,

00:37:39.850 --> 00:37:43.360
and recognizes that
for every organism,

00:37:43.360 --> 00:37:46.270
there is a problem to the
solution of which it is ideally

00:37:46.270 --> 00:37:47.740
suited.

00:37:47.740 --> 00:37:49.270
And that is how I address ants.

00:37:49.270 --> 00:37:52.830
Now let me go back in time
to the start of my career.

00:37:52.830 --> 00:37:57.700
I've just graduated from
Harvard, a PhD, 1955,

00:37:57.700 --> 00:38:01.330
and I'm in the South Pacific,
in New Guinea and the islands

00:38:01.330 --> 00:38:02.870
of the South Pacific.

00:38:02.870 --> 00:38:07.640
And I'm doing this kind of study
then, totally absorbed in it,

00:38:07.640 --> 00:38:09.760
and the habitats in
which they lived.

00:38:09.760 --> 00:38:15.670
And I am thus coming
intimately to know

00:38:15.670 --> 00:38:17.670
hundreds of species of ants.

00:38:17.670 --> 00:38:21.530
And out of this, I'm
finding patterns.

00:38:21.530 --> 00:38:24.290
The evolutionary biologist
is a typical example

00:38:24.290 --> 00:38:27.470
of the person who
goes to the organism

00:38:27.470 --> 00:38:31.040
and derives the solution of
problems to which it's ideally

00:38:31.040 --> 00:38:33.904
suited and often perceives
the problem, what

00:38:33.904 --> 00:38:35.570
it is, in the first
place, because we're

00:38:35.570 --> 00:38:37.010
looking for pattern.

00:38:37.010 --> 00:38:39.740
And out of this work,
I see two things.

00:38:39.740 --> 00:38:43.310
I see the patterns in which
the species are flowing out

00:38:43.310 --> 00:38:48.930
of the Indo-Australian
staging areas

00:38:48.930 --> 00:38:52.210
into New Guinea and the
outer islands of Melanesia.

00:38:52.210 --> 00:38:53.420
Just flow.

00:38:53.420 --> 00:38:55.760
And I see the circumstances
under which species

00:38:55.760 --> 00:38:58.510
can spread most
readily, and I develop

00:38:58.510 --> 00:38:59.910
a broad picture of that.

00:38:59.910 --> 00:39:02.060
And the second
thing that I see is

00:39:02.060 --> 00:39:04.250
that there's a very
regular relationship

00:39:04.250 --> 00:39:06.440
between the number of
species on the island

00:39:06.440 --> 00:39:08.050
and the area of the island.

00:39:08.050 --> 00:39:11.750
And out of this, to make a long
story short, later, I come back

00:39:11.750 --> 00:39:14.630
and I collaborate with a
young mathematical ecologist

00:39:14.630 --> 00:39:16.180
named Robert MacArthur.

00:39:16.180 --> 00:39:19.160
And we developed a theory of
island biogeography, which

00:39:19.160 --> 00:39:23.240
is an equilibrium theory of
species in which it leads us

00:39:23.240 --> 00:39:26.360
into the concept and the
processes of immigration

00:39:26.360 --> 00:39:31.500
and extinction and so on and
connects it with ecology.

00:39:31.500 --> 00:39:36.440
Now, let me, having said that--

00:39:36.440 --> 00:39:39.530
and mentioning that many,
many discoveries in biology

00:39:39.530 --> 00:39:43.100
are made this way, by solving
problems that were never

00:39:43.100 --> 00:39:47.090
dreamed of until you learn
to live with the organism,

00:39:47.090 --> 00:39:48.556
to move to--

00:39:48.556 --> 00:39:49.430
how much time have I?

00:39:49.430 --> 00:39:51.410
LIGHTMAN: You've got
about five minutes.

00:39:51.410 --> 00:39:52.600
WILSON: Five minutes.

00:39:52.600 --> 00:39:55.460
To move to the image
of the Earth rise.

00:39:55.460 --> 00:40:03.840
And what do I see when I look
at that distant planet, Susan?

00:40:03.840 --> 00:40:09.204
What I do, first of all, because
I'm a biogeographer by nature,

00:40:09.204 --> 00:40:10.620
the first thing
automatically I do

00:40:10.620 --> 00:40:13.500
when I see these distant
photographs, I say,

00:40:13.500 --> 00:40:15.640
how do the continents look?

00:40:15.640 --> 00:40:18.150
And are those maps we've
been using accurate?

00:40:18.150 --> 00:40:20.220
At last, we can find out.

00:40:20.220 --> 00:40:25.020
And then, of course, I see
it as the astronauts see it.

00:40:25.020 --> 00:40:28.290
I see it as a distant
and fragile planet.

00:40:28.290 --> 00:40:31.730
And then I say, but what
do we mean by fragile?

00:40:31.730 --> 00:40:32.580
It's not fragile.

00:40:32.580 --> 00:40:34.990
What's fragile is the biosphere.

00:40:34.990 --> 00:40:37.890
The biosphere is that layer
of multitudinous organisms

00:40:37.890 --> 00:40:43.740
that envelops it pole to pole
and on which our lives depend.

00:40:43.740 --> 00:40:47.640
And that biosphere,
an astronomical number

00:40:47.640 --> 00:40:52.620
of organisms of some 10
million species, perhaps,

00:40:52.620 --> 00:40:54.210
creates a disequilibrium.

00:40:54.210 --> 00:41:02.880
It's an atmosphere and
a temperature regime

00:41:02.880 --> 00:41:05.370
which is out of
equilibrium from what

00:41:05.370 --> 00:41:08.520
it would be if the organisms
were not there driving it

00:41:08.520 --> 00:41:10.020
to a new point.

00:41:10.020 --> 00:41:12.280
And that's terribly fragile.

00:41:12.280 --> 00:41:16.440
You look at that and you
tried to see the biosphere.

00:41:16.440 --> 00:41:18.420
You cannot.

00:41:18.420 --> 00:41:21.000
You can't even see it
from a space shuttle.

00:41:21.000 --> 00:41:22.860
It's razor thin.

00:41:22.860 --> 00:41:26.760
It cannot be seen with
the naked eye edgewise.

00:41:26.760 --> 00:41:31.620
And we now know, from
many lines of evidence,

00:41:31.620 --> 00:41:34.800
that we are disturbing
the biosphere

00:41:34.800 --> 00:41:37.290
in a way that is almost
unprecedented, at least

00:41:37.290 --> 00:41:39.840
in recent geologic history.

00:41:39.840 --> 00:41:43.590
And that we have become
a geological force.

00:41:43.590 --> 00:41:49.770
So let's go to the progression
from Australopithecus

00:41:49.770 --> 00:41:52.860
on the left there up
through the species of Homo

00:41:52.860 --> 00:41:55.050
to Homo sapiens.

00:41:55.050 --> 00:41:58.830
And Homo sapiens is
the great destroyer.

00:41:58.830 --> 00:42:01.620
And incidentally,
an interesting fact

00:42:01.620 --> 00:42:05.430
is that we've estimated that
at any given time on Earth,

00:42:05.430 --> 00:42:14.430
there are about 1,000 trillion
individual ants alive.

00:42:14.430 --> 00:42:18.749
And each ant weighs about one
millionth of a human being.

00:42:18.749 --> 00:42:20.790
And that means that there
are approximately about

00:42:20.790 --> 00:42:25.140
as much biomass of ants
as there are humans.

00:42:25.140 --> 00:42:29.370
Now, this is really abnormal
for the following reason.

00:42:29.370 --> 00:42:33.990
If all humans were to
disappear, everything

00:42:33.990 --> 00:42:36.434
would come back to the
equilibrium, approximately,

00:42:36.434 --> 00:42:38.100
and that planet would
be-- shall we say,

00:42:38.100 --> 00:42:42.300
the biosphere would be safe
for the indefinite future.

00:42:42.300 --> 00:42:44.390
But if all ants
were to disappear,

00:42:44.390 --> 00:42:46.530
the terrestrial
ecosystems would collapse.

00:42:46.530 --> 00:42:49.050
It would be a catastrophe,
and the whole thing

00:42:49.050 --> 00:42:52.428
would be thrown out of kilter.

00:42:52.428 --> 00:42:55.284
[APPLAUSE]

00:42:56.240 --> 00:43:01.480
So out of our knowledge--
to conclude with something

00:43:01.480 --> 00:43:05.350
of a rhetorical note--

00:43:05.350 --> 00:43:07.810
that we have learned by all
of these painful methods

00:43:07.810 --> 00:43:09.920
of the natural
sciences, in particular,

00:43:09.920 --> 00:43:12.340
the studies of the
diversity of life,

00:43:12.340 --> 00:43:15.634
has come the realization
that we've got to--

00:43:15.634 --> 00:43:17.550
somehow, we've got to
settle down before we've

00:43:17.550 --> 00:43:18.341
wrecked the planet.

00:43:21.250 --> 00:43:23.482
LIGHTMAN: Thank you

00:43:23.482 --> 00:43:26.440
[APPLAUSE]

00:43:32.860 --> 00:43:37.050
Well, let's have some
back and forth now.

00:43:37.050 --> 00:43:38.801
Any of you have--

00:43:38.801 --> 00:43:42.700
WILSON: Why don't we
ask Susan, if I might?

00:43:42.700 --> 00:43:45.789
Have either one of us
gotten close to what

00:43:45.789 --> 00:43:47.080
she wants us to do with images?

00:43:50.104 --> 00:43:51.520
SONTAG: I don't
want to be cranky,

00:43:51.520 --> 00:43:55.360
but I just think that
these images are just

00:43:55.360 --> 00:44:00.717
inviting us to free associate
about things that we

00:44:00.717 --> 00:44:01.800
think of when we look at--

00:44:01.800 --> 00:44:05.510
I mean, I look at that
image, you know, and I say,

00:44:05.510 --> 00:44:06.035
it's a man.

00:44:06.035 --> 00:44:07.700
It's not a woman.

00:44:07.700 --> 00:44:08.670
Isn't it interesting?

00:44:08.670 --> 00:44:11.704
It's a man.

00:44:11.704 --> 00:44:14.190
[APPLAUSE]

00:44:14.690 --> 00:44:19.070
Well, I belong to that half
of the human race that is not

00:44:19.070 --> 00:44:23.660
likely to be represented in a
representative chart of, let's

00:44:23.660 --> 00:44:26.450
say, human evolution.

00:44:26.450 --> 00:44:28.850
Whereas, those of you
who are men probably

00:44:28.850 --> 00:44:32.990
find it quite normal, just
as he stands for everybody,

00:44:32.990 --> 00:44:37.240
but she is kind of weird
to stand for everybody.

00:44:37.240 --> 00:44:40.430
Let's say I bring
that to that image.

00:44:40.430 --> 00:44:42.920
But at the same time
that I do sincerely

00:44:42.920 --> 00:44:45.770
notice that, that it is,
in fact, a male and not

00:44:45.770 --> 00:44:49.370
a female, which could, after
all, just as easily illustrate

00:44:49.370 --> 00:44:54.140
that evolution, I think it is,
to use a word that is both used

00:44:54.140 --> 00:44:55.730
and withdrawn at
the same time, it's

00:44:55.730 --> 00:44:57.690
kind of frivolous observation.

00:44:57.690 --> 00:45:00.050
It's just an
association that I have

00:45:00.050 --> 00:45:03.230
because of my particular
ethical sensibility, et cetera.

00:45:03.230 --> 00:45:06.110
One I'm sure is shared by many
other people, needless to say.

00:45:06.110 --> 00:45:08.510
But it is simply an association.

00:45:08.510 --> 00:45:12.180
We could have associations
to all of these images.

00:45:12.180 --> 00:45:17.480
But I'm not sure associations,
using images as a pretext

00:45:17.480 --> 00:45:23.960
to associate and present what we
think or feel or want to argue,

00:45:23.960 --> 00:45:27.020
is a very interesting
response to images.

00:45:27.020 --> 00:45:31.100
Because this is precisely
a little, tiny anthology.

00:45:31.100 --> 00:45:35.210
It's an anthology of famous
or easily recognizable images.

00:45:35.210 --> 00:45:37.520
I think would be more
interesting to talk

00:45:37.520 --> 00:45:44.270
about how the visual has
become so important for us.

00:45:47.540 --> 00:45:49.860
Alan Lightman and I,
who are both novelists,

00:45:49.860 --> 00:45:52.790
we're having a long discussion
in the middle of the afternoon

00:45:52.790 --> 00:45:55.000
about the difference--

00:45:55.000 --> 00:45:59.120
it's something that
preoccupies, I think,

00:45:59.120 --> 00:46:01.117
reflective writers
of fiction very much,

00:46:01.117 --> 00:46:03.200
the difference between
writing in the first person

00:46:03.200 --> 00:46:05.090
and writing in the third person.

00:46:05.090 --> 00:46:08.360
We could also have
a conversation

00:46:08.360 --> 00:46:11.150
about the difference in
photographed images as opposed

00:46:11.150 --> 00:46:13.130
to drawn images.

00:46:13.130 --> 00:46:16.350
We could have a conversation
about the difference

00:46:16.350 --> 00:46:20.840
between still images and moving
images, et cetera, et cetera.

00:46:20.840 --> 00:46:26.930
Those, I think, would focus
us on how we think visually,

00:46:26.930 --> 00:46:32.300
how we remember visually, the
way in which visualizing is

00:46:32.300 --> 00:46:34.100
essential to the imagination.

00:46:34.100 --> 00:46:37.460
And it's essential,
can become essential,

00:46:37.460 --> 00:46:39.680
in pedagogy and understanding.

00:46:39.680 --> 00:46:45.910
But simply to respond
to individual images,

00:46:45.910 --> 00:46:50.540
I find, not getting very far to
the kinds of problems that are

00:46:50.540 --> 00:46:52.880
really, I think, interesting.

00:46:52.880 --> 00:46:55.030
PENROSE: I don't really
understand your point--

00:46:55.030 --> 00:46:57.185
is this working--
about your objection

00:46:57.185 --> 00:46:58.310
to these particular images.

00:46:58.310 --> 00:47:00.860
I mean, in particular
that one, which actually

00:47:00.860 --> 00:47:02.220
isn't a photograph, of course.

00:47:02.220 --> 00:47:03.490
The one on the
left at the bottom.

00:47:03.490 --> 00:47:04.150
SONTAG: No, it's
not a photograph.

00:47:04.150 --> 00:47:04.670
You're quite right.

00:47:04.670 --> 00:47:06.080
PENROSE: Which evoked
a response from you.

00:47:06.080 --> 00:47:06.800
Fine.

00:47:06.800 --> 00:47:09.200
I think that's the sort of
thing he was trying to do.

00:47:09.200 --> 00:47:11.870
OK, these are images
which, in different ways,

00:47:11.870 --> 00:47:13.300
can evoke responses from us.

00:47:13.300 --> 00:47:13.680
And I think that's
something valuable.

00:47:13.680 --> 00:47:15.800
SONTAG: No, my
objection is not--

00:47:15.800 --> 00:47:19.550
my objection is that I did
think we were being invited

00:47:19.550 --> 00:47:22.850
to reflect on the
role of the visual

00:47:22.850 --> 00:47:26.510
in helping us to understand.

00:47:26.510 --> 00:47:30.590
And therefore, I find
responses to individual images,

00:47:30.590 --> 00:47:32.920
because they trigger
associations--

00:47:32.920 --> 00:47:35.210
whether they're my associations
or your associations,

00:47:35.210 --> 00:47:36.530
that's not the point--

00:47:36.530 --> 00:47:39.320
not to take us very far.

00:47:39.320 --> 00:47:42.050
PENROSE: What kind of
would you prefer to have?

00:47:42.050 --> 00:47:43.694
What would you
have put up there?

00:47:43.694 --> 00:47:45.110
SONTAG: Well,
perhaps which were--

00:47:45.110 --> 00:47:46.220
WILSON: And what
would you understand?

00:47:46.220 --> 00:47:48.680
SONTAG: Perhaps images which
all related to one subject.

00:47:48.680 --> 00:47:50.390
Look, listen, we
have a precedent.

00:47:50.390 --> 00:47:52.340
Let's talk very concretely.

00:47:52.340 --> 00:47:57.470
Darwin writes a book called
The Expression of Emotions

00:47:57.470 --> 00:48:00.980
in Man and Animals, and he uses
a bunch of photographs, which

00:48:00.980 --> 00:48:03.770
are largely faked or staged.

00:48:03.770 --> 00:48:06.660
And yet, he didn't think
that that was a useful thing.

00:48:06.660 --> 00:48:10.850
And we're talking
about 1872, I believe

00:48:10.850 --> 00:48:13.640
that book is, 1872, 1873.

00:48:13.640 --> 00:48:17.210
It's an early example
of somebody thinking

00:48:17.210 --> 00:48:20.780
that visual aids, in
particular photographed images,

00:48:20.780 --> 00:48:25.550
would be helpful in
making clear a scientific

00:48:25.550 --> 00:48:29.210
or a descriptive or
a taxonomic argument.

00:48:29.210 --> 00:48:30.890
I mean, I find that
very interesting

00:48:30.890 --> 00:48:35.040
that it happened that long ago.

00:48:35.040 --> 00:48:36.950
We know, of course,
the Encyclopedia,

00:48:36.950 --> 00:48:39.800
the great encyclopedic project
of the late 18th century,

00:48:39.800 --> 00:48:40.520
had images.

00:48:40.520 --> 00:48:42.560
Obviously, they were
drawn images and not

00:48:42.560 --> 00:48:45.990
photographic images.

00:48:45.990 --> 00:48:47.930
Anyway, I think that's
an interesting thing

00:48:47.930 --> 00:48:50.860
to talk about.

00:48:50.860 --> 00:48:54.140
For instance, here's an
obvious question and one

00:48:54.140 --> 00:48:58.820
that troubles me, is
the rise of such images

00:48:58.820 --> 00:49:00.920
and the frequency
with which they

00:49:00.920 --> 00:49:06.410
occur in books and scientific
and historical works

00:49:06.410 --> 00:49:09.350
a part of a kind
of democratization

00:49:09.350 --> 00:49:11.990
of popularization
of knowledge or not?

00:49:11.990 --> 00:49:18.880
Is the feeling that an argument
isn't compelling to people

00:49:18.880 --> 00:49:21.700
unless it has a
visual accompaniment--

00:49:21.700 --> 00:49:23.290
I mean, haven't
you all been struck

00:49:23.290 --> 00:49:29.530
by the extent to which
pictures, news pictures,

00:49:29.530 --> 00:49:33.280
have come onto the front
page of newspapers,

00:49:33.280 --> 00:49:35.320
of newspapers which
didn't feature them?

00:49:35.320 --> 00:49:39.430
The extent to which we now
view black and white pictures

00:49:39.430 --> 00:49:41.830
as not appropriate
in newspapers,

00:49:41.830 --> 00:49:46.300
but rather, that they should be
in color, et cetera, et cetera.

00:49:46.300 --> 00:49:49.540
Why have we, more
and more, wanted

00:49:49.540 --> 00:49:52.690
to accompany narratives--
political narratives,

00:49:52.690 --> 00:49:55.690
scientific narratives,
descriptive accounts--

00:49:55.690 --> 00:49:58.720
with visual information?

00:49:58.720 --> 00:50:03.260
I think this has a sociological
and political aspect.

00:50:03.260 --> 00:50:04.030
I don't know.

00:50:04.030 --> 00:50:06.404
That's just the kind of thing
that I think about, anyway.

00:50:08.800 --> 00:50:11.860
WILSON: May I suggest another
connection, a wholly different

00:50:11.860 --> 00:50:15.610
direction that might help
illuminate this, that has

00:50:15.610 --> 00:50:18.130
been yielded by neurobiology?

00:50:18.130 --> 00:50:20.770
It's just a tentative
on the basis of one

00:50:20.770 --> 00:50:22.680
set of experiments I know.

00:50:22.680 --> 00:50:27.380
And that is arousal of the
brain, automatic arousal,

00:50:27.380 --> 00:50:30.810
which you may not
even be conscious of.

00:50:30.810 --> 00:50:33.340
It's a measure of the dampening
of the alpha wave, which

00:50:33.340 --> 00:50:35.980
is a good proxy for--

00:50:35.980 --> 00:50:38.800
well, actually, it's directly
connected with arousal.

00:50:38.800 --> 00:50:41.230
And the experiments
seem to indicate

00:50:41.230 --> 00:50:45.810
that if we were to turn each
one of these into abstract form

00:50:45.810 --> 00:50:49.150
so that they weren't
recognizable images

00:50:49.150 --> 00:50:52.870
in our ordinary lives
but make them abstract,

00:50:52.870 --> 00:50:56.080
it's very likely that the fetus
would cause maximum arousal

00:50:56.080 --> 00:50:58.480
and to be pretty close
to a spike in how

00:50:58.480 --> 00:51:00.460
you were automatically aroused.

00:51:00.460 --> 00:51:05.330
And that would be a figure
with about 20% redundancy.

00:51:05.330 --> 00:51:07.600
And that's about the
amount of complexity

00:51:07.600 --> 00:51:11.070
you see in that fetal diagram.

00:51:11.070 --> 00:51:15.820
And that happens to
be where we settle

00:51:15.820 --> 00:51:18.149
in a great deal of abstract art.

00:51:18.149 --> 00:51:19.690
That may be a
coincidence, but that's

00:51:19.690 --> 00:51:23.560
where Mondrian settled after
all his experimentation

00:51:23.560 --> 00:51:25.690
and movement away
from literalism.

00:51:25.690 --> 00:51:30.130
And it also is the
amount of complexity

00:51:30.130 --> 00:51:34.360
you see in Asian pictographs
and in glyph characters

00:51:34.360 --> 00:51:38.170
and in frieze design
and in coliforms.

00:51:38.170 --> 00:51:45.730
And in fact, it's what
automatically we pay attention

00:51:45.730 --> 00:51:50.420
to instantaneously more than
other degrees of complexity.

00:51:50.420 --> 00:51:51.670
So it's just a thought.

00:51:51.670 --> 00:51:53.860
In other words, what
does this tell us

00:51:53.860 --> 00:51:59.230
about image and meaning?

00:51:59.230 --> 00:52:01.550
I think it probably tells
us something important.

00:52:01.550 --> 00:52:04.540
It certainly tells
us where what might

00:52:04.540 --> 00:52:08.710
be a gravitational
force in the evolution

00:52:08.710 --> 00:52:12.010
of our so-called
primitive art, design.

00:52:12.010 --> 00:52:15.460
Is it about that 20%
redundancy level?

00:52:15.460 --> 00:52:16.599
Just an observation.

00:52:16.599 --> 00:52:18.890
PENROSE: I think you raised
a point which I don't think

00:52:18.890 --> 00:52:20.348
has been-- at least,
I haven't seen

00:52:20.348 --> 00:52:22.510
it discussed at this meeting.

00:52:22.510 --> 00:52:24.880
The question of cartoons and
that sort of thing, where

00:52:24.880 --> 00:52:28.010
you can convey an impression.

00:52:28.010 --> 00:52:31.600
I mean, not photographic, but
with much less information,

00:52:31.600 --> 00:52:32.350
if you like.

00:52:32.350 --> 00:52:35.350
But nevertheless, you can
instantly recognize what it is.

00:52:35.350 --> 00:52:36.850
And as you say,
with that image, you

00:52:36.850 --> 00:52:38.808
could have done that
probably with a few lines.

00:52:38.808 --> 00:52:40.940
WILSON: That's what we
call diagnostic traits

00:52:40.940 --> 00:52:42.730
in systematics.

00:52:42.730 --> 00:52:45.490
We use it in a field guides
like Peterson's field guide.

00:52:45.490 --> 00:52:46.990
But there's another
aspect, too, I'd

00:52:46.990 --> 00:52:51.250
like to mention that has come
out of the subject of ethology,

00:52:51.250 --> 00:52:55.080
which is experimentation,
experimental natural behavior

00:52:55.080 --> 00:52:56.170
study.

00:52:56.170 --> 00:52:57.960
And that's a
supernormal stimulus.

00:52:57.960 --> 00:53:01.700
I'm reminded of that by your
allusion to cartoonists.

00:53:01.700 --> 00:53:07.240
Cartooning is a
substantial process,

00:53:07.240 --> 00:53:10.720
the exaggeration of certain
features that release--

00:53:10.720 --> 00:53:12.280
that is, stimulate us--

00:53:12.280 --> 00:53:13.210
in a certain way.

00:53:13.210 --> 00:53:16.330
Emotionally, perhaps,
through cultural mediation.

00:53:16.330 --> 00:53:18.140
Sometimes automatically.

00:53:18.140 --> 00:53:21.650
So we go for the
infant, for example.

00:53:21.650 --> 00:53:25.220
You exaggerate the amount
that the nose is pushed in

00:53:25.220 --> 00:53:28.210
and the eyes grow large
and the body of the head

00:53:28.210 --> 00:53:32.260
is disproportionately
large for the infant's form

00:53:32.260 --> 00:53:35.500
and the body and so on.

00:53:35.500 --> 00:53:41.200
And when we present horrific
figures, either in horror films

00:53:41.200 --> 00:53:47.350
or in our meaning to dramatize
the horrors of war and so on,

00:53:47.350 --> 00:53:49.420
we use supernormal stimuli.

00:53:49.420 --> 00:53:53.170
This means that in
certain stimuli,

00:53:53.170 --> 00:53:57.450
certain categories of stimuli,
exaggerating the features

00:53:57.450 --> 00:54:02.570
that you would find in a normal
context would-- from predators,

00:54:02.570 --> 00:54:05.470
from children, for what
we consider beautiful men

00:54:05.470 --> 00:54:06.290
or women.

00:54:06.290 --> 00:54:11.290
By exaggerating them, you get
an even stronger response.

00:54:11.290 --> 00:54:15.650
SONTAG: Don't you think, also,
that it has to do with framing?

00:54:15.650 --> 00:54:22.032
I recently saw an exhibit
of war photography,

00:54:22.032 --> 00:54:23.990
because I am very interested
in this right now.

00:54:23.990 --> 00:54:25.370
I'm trying to write something.

00:54:25.370 --> 00:54:27.560
In which you saw the
original photograph and then

00:54:27.560 --> 00:54:29.590
you saw the
photograph as printed.

00:54:29.590 --> 00:54:31.340
For instance, that
very famous photograph

00:54:31.340 --> 00:54:33.800
from the Vietnam War,
probably the most famous

00:54:33.800 --> 00:54:38.120
single photograph, of the
naked child running down

00:54:38.120 --> 00:54:41.880
the highway toward the
camera who's been napalmed.

00:54:41.880 --> 00:54:43.796
It's actually a
much larger image,

00:54:43.796 --> 00:54:45.170
and I've seen the
original image.

00:54:45.170 --> 00:54:50.030
And of course, it's shrunk so
that she's really in the center

00:54:50.030 --> 00:54:51.330
so you can't miss her.

00:54:51.330 --> 00:54:54.450
On the other hand, the larger
picture is also very upsetting,

00:54:54.450 --> 00:54:56.660
because it shows quite
a few people, including

00:54:56.660 --> 00:54:58.280
American soldiers--

00:54:58.280 --> 00:55:00.200
mostly American
soldiers-- standing

00:55:00.200 --> 00:55:02.750
on the side of the road not
paying any attention at all.

00:55:02.750 --> 00:55:05.750
One is talking to another one.

00:55:05.750 --> 00:55:08.157
And this child, who
must be screaming--

00:55:08.157 --> 00:55:10.490
as well as there are other
children in the picture, too.

00:55:10.490 --> 00:55:11.864
They're not paying
any attention.

00:55:11.864 --> 00:55:14.480
But nevertheless, we
would look differently

00:55:14.480 --> 00:55:17.360
if the picture-- and it would
have had a different impact.

00:55:17.360 --> 00:55:19.160
So it's not just exaggeration.

00:55:19.160 --> 00:55:26.690
But I think one can never ignore
framing, what is excluded,

00:55:26.690 --> 00:55:30.590
and how information is
centered in a picture.

00:55:30.590 --> 00:55:34.370
WILSON: Let me make a quick
allusion to that final image.

00:55:34.370 --> 00:55:38.970
And that is where our cognitive
neuroscience may be leading us.

00:55:38.970 --> 00:55:43.340
And that is that a
lot of these appear

00:55:43.340 --> 00:55:46.970
to be fundamental traits,
the way the brain and the way

00:55:46.970 --> 00:55:51.200
we respond to certain types of
stimuli, contexts, and so on.

00:55:51.200 --> 00:55:53.510
A lot of that appears
to be programmed.

00:55:53.510 --> 00:55:57.200
And how much it is and how much
can be culturally modified,

00:55:57.200 --> 00:56:00.710
I think, is going to be
understood a great deal more

00:56:00.710 --> 00:56:03.170
as time goes on with
the aid of techniques

00:56:03.170 --> 00:56:04.490
like the one displayed here.

00:56:04.490 --> 00:56:06.210
I just wanted to
mention that in passing.

00:56:06.210 --> 00:56:09.080
And what that means
is I'm not sure,

00:56:09.080 --> 00:56:11.420
but it certainly
does allude to the--

00:56:11.420 --> 00:56:14.900
or is relevant to the general
topic of tonight, which

00:56:14.900 --> 00:56:17.199
is the relation between
image and meaning.

00:56:17.199 --> 00:56:19.490
LIGHTMAN: I wanted to ask
both of you a question that's

00:56:19.490 --> 00:56:22.880
sort of perpendicular to Susan's
comment that a lot of images

00:56:22.880 --> 00:56:25.850
are just celebrity
images and bring forth

00:56:25.850 --> 00:56:29.030
a canned set of associations.

00:56:29.030 --> 00:56:32.180
Both of you use
images in your work.

00:56:32.180 --> 00:56:34.700
I mean, Ed, you
mentioned that you

00:56:34.700 --> 00:56:40.790
are drawing 5,000 parts
of ants or whole ants.

00:56:40.790 --> 00:56:44.750
And I know Roger,
among all physicists,

00:56:44.750 --> 00:56:49.080
uses images to an
unusual degree--

00:56:49.080 --> 00:56:54.140
geometric constructions,
which you must visualize.

00:56:54.140 --> 00:56:59.000
How do these images
actually help you

00:56:59.000 --> 00:57:01.220
in your scientific work--

00:57:01.220 --> 00:57:06.500
the visualization, in your case,
Roger, and the actual drawing

00:57:06.500 --> 00:57:09.690
by hand, in your case, Ed?

00:57:09.690 --> 00:57:12.320
PENROSE: Well,
certainly, I find it

00:57:12.320 --> 00:57:15.540
very useful in understanding
mathematical things.

00:57:15.540 --> 00:57:19.610
I mean, in my notebooks, I
tend to draw lots of pictures,

00:57:19.610 --> 00:57:22.900
far more than I would
actually have calculations.

00:57:22.900 --> 00:57:24.980
Once a problem is
reduced to a calculation,

00:57:24.980 --> 00:57:28.110
I feel it's almost
solved, because then you

00:57:28.110 --> 00:57:29.830
can just write it down.

00:57:29.830 --> 00:57:32.120
Whereas, it's the
conceptual ideas

00:57:32.120 --> 00:57:36.050
which are very difficult to get
to grips with without having

00:57:36.050 --> 00:57:37.880
some kind of visual image.

00:57:37.880 --> 00:57:42.169
Often, it's very difficult
to have accurate images.

00:57:42.169 --> 00:57:43.960
Even people who don't
think very visually--

00:57:43.960 --> 00:57:46.520
there's a famous little
book by Hadamard,

00:57:46.520 --> 00:57:50.390
where he writes on how
mathematicians think and so on.

00:57:50.390 --> 00:57:54.860
And he describes his ideas, and
he has these blobs and things

00:57:54.860 --> 00:57:55.792
like this.

00:57:55.792 --> 00:57:58.000
And then he stands back and
thinks about it and says,

00:57:58.000 --> 00:58:00.624
well, these blobs don't actually
have much information in them.

00:58:00.624 --> 00:58:03.010
And he regards himself as
not on the visual side.

00:58:03.010 --> 00:58:05.270
He's a sort of
analytical thinker.

00:58:05.270 --> 00:58:09.167
So these images
are very important

00:58:09.167 --> 00:58:10.250
for mathematical thinking.

00:58:10.250 --> 00:58:14.600
I think Keith Devlin, whose talk
yesterday brought up this issue

00:58:14.600 --> 00:58:20.000
that bringing up visual
images in actual mathematical

00:58:20.000 --> 00:58:23.300
demonstrations for
other mathematicians

00:58:23.300 --> 00:58:26.030
is often regarded as
being kind of inferior.

00:58:26.030 --> 00:58:28.030
And you should try to
get rid of the diagrams.

00:58:28.030 --> 00:58:29.613
You can do them for
your own purposes.

00:58:29.613 --> 00:58:32.930
But when you want to
make an honest article,

00:58:32.930 --> 00:58:34.100
you don't put them in.

00:58:34.100 --> 00:58:37.080
I think that things have moved
away from that, to some degree.

00:58:37.080 --> 00:58:39.950
The pendulum swings
one way and the other.

00:58:39.950 --> 00:58:42.720
And I think images are
used quite a bit more

00:58:42.720 --> 00:58:46.330
in very advanced mathematical
articles sometimes.

00:58:46.330 --> 00:58:49.280
It depends on the subject.

00:58:49.280 --> 00:58:51.432
The problem with
these visual images

00:58:51.432 --> 00:58:52.640
is how to make them rigorous.

00:58:52.640 --> 00:58:56.180
And mathematicians want to make
sure the arguments actually

00:58:56.180 --> 00:58:58.640
hang together in
a very strict way.

00:58:58.640 --> 00:59:01.590
And the images may be thought
be distracting from that.

00:59:01.590 --> 00:59:05.759
But I think in the
exploratory thinking,

00:59:05.759 --> 00:59:06.800
they're absolutely vital.

00:59:06.800 --> 00:59:08.120
I found them vital.

00:59:08.120 --> 00:59:10.760
I don't know to what
degree other people who

00:59:10.760 --> 00:59:13.910
work in mathematics find this--
probably less, because I happen

00:59:13.910 --> 00:59:16.684
to be on the visual side.

00:59:16.684 --> 00:59:18.100
That's just in
one's own thinking.

00:59:18.100 --> 00:59:22.090
But then, of course, in
explaining to other people,

00:59:22.090 --> 00:59:23.560
they are extremely important.

00:59:23.560 --> 00:59:29.260
It's quite curious that
in mathematics teaching,

00:59:29.260 --> 00:59:32.750
you will often find that
mathematic students find

00:59:32.750 --> 00:59:35.050
the diagrams not very helpful.

00:59:35.050 --> 00:59:37.730
And I think there's a
curious reason for that.

00:59:37.730 --> 00:59:40.270
And that is that there's a
kind of selection effect,

00:59:40.270 --> 00:59:42.700
that it's very hard
to have examinations

00:59:42.700 --> 00:59:44.830
which test the visual ability.

00:59:44.830 --> 00:59:47.770
Whereas, to test the analytic
and calculation abilities,

00:59:47.770 --> 00:59:48.647
that's easy.

00:59:48.647 --> 00:59:50.980
And so the people who do well
on that side come through,

00:59:50.980 --> 00:59:53.290
do well in the exams, and
you find a lot of them

00:59:53.290 --> 00:59:54.910
in math classes.

00:59:54.910 --> 00:59:57.710
Whereas maybe the best ones,
or the one who come through,

00:59:57.710 --> 01:00:00.520
will be able to do things
on the visual side, too.

01:00:00.520 --> 01:00:04.499
But you find that, on the whole,
the mathematics students--

01:00:04.499 --> 01:00:06.040
at least, that's
been my experience--

01:00:06.040 --> 01:00:08.530
are not at all visual.

01:00:08.530 --> 01:00:11.470
Now, it's a curious thing,
because if somebody says to me,

01:00:11.470 --> 01:00:16.600
OK, we want you to do
some public lecture

01:00:16.600 --> 01:00:19.430
or something like this where
you can to talk to people

01:00:19.430 --> 01:00:20.650
who are not mathematicians.

01:00:20.650 --> 01:00:21.800
Lots and lots of pictures.

01:00:21.800 --> 01:00:22.972
Lots of pictures, they say.

01:00:22.972 --> 01:00:25.180
And that's the way you get
the ideas across to people

01:00:25.180 --> 01:00:26.826
who are not mathematicians.

01:00:26.826 --> 01:00:28.450
LIGHTMAN: It works
at the two extremes.

01:00:28.450 --> 01:00:30.310
PENROSE: Yes.

01:00:30.310 --> 01:00:31.950
I mean, I find they're
very important.

01:00:31.950 --> 01:00:34.900
I think they are extremely
valuable in getting ideas

01:00:34.900 --> 01:00:36.970
across to people who
are not mathematicians.

01:00:36.970 --> 01:00:39.940
Probably more so than
with mathematicians.

01:00:39.940 --> 01:00:41.470
But on the other
hand, I am somebody

01:00:41.470 --> 01:00:45.160
who believes that they
have a very big value

01:00:45.160 --> 01:00:47.630
within mathematics, too.

01:00:47.630 --> 01:00:50.730
But for me, they're
absolutely essential.

01:00:50.730 --> 01:00:53.410
LIGHTMAN: Do you feel like
you know your ants better

01:00:53.410 --> 01:00:55.130
after drawing them?

01:00:55.130 --> 01:00:57.250
WILSON: Oh, certainly.

01:00:57.250 --> 01:00:59.110
You just gain that
much more familiarity

01:00:59.110 --> 01:01:00.484
and then affix it
into your mind.

01:01:00.484 --> 01:01:04.590
But watching Roger
using movements,

01:01:04.590 --> 01:01:06.430
paralinguistic signaling--

01:01:06.430 --> 01:01:11.980
and then the dinner
conversation,

01:01:11.980 --> 01:01:15.760
trying to explain an important
principle of quantum mechanics

01:01:15.760 --> 01:01:21.180
to a admittedly baffled
dinner companion.

01:01:21.180 --> 01:01:23.680
It was very effectively
using objects

01:01:23.680 --> 01:01:24.950
on the table to move around.

01:01:24.950 --> 01:01:28.900
And I was reminded then
of the greater importance

01:01:28.900 --> 01:01:35.310
of narrative motion and
kinesthetic involvement.

01:01:35.310 --> 01:01:36.290
Kinesthetic.

01:01:36.290 --> 01:01:38.620
You know, we are
not just creatures

01:01:38.620 --> 01:01:41.540
of the ordinary
senses we think of.

01:01:41.540 --> 01:01:45.130
We're also creatures
exquisitely adapted

01:01:45.130 --> 01:01:48.090
to doing things,
especially with our hands,

01:01:48.090 --> 01:01:53.600
and creating and producing
products and so on.

01:01:53.600 --> 01:01:56.945
So this is very pleasing
to us, to use our hands

01:01:56.945 --> 01:01:58.490
and to work with them.

01:01:58.490 --> 01:02:02.890
And this is extremely
true of biologists

01:02:02.890 --> 01:02:06.010
who work with whole organisms.

01:02:06.010 --> 01:02:09.580
So yes, it's the kinesthetic
element, actually doing it--

01:02:09.580 --> 01:02:12.670
PENROSE: That came up yesterday,
incidentally, with the--

01:02:12.670 --> 01:02:14.690
sometimes, in the
architectural discussion,

01:02:14.690 --> 01:02:17.074
with producing actual models
you can get your hands on.

01:02:17.074 --> 01:02:17.740
WILSON: Exactly.

01:02:17.740 --> 01:02:20.950
It's aesthetically pleasing.

01:02:20.950 --> 01:02:25.510
It enhances learning, and it
certainly enhances creativity.

01:02:25.510 --> 01:02:28.060
And I'd like to ask you
a question about that.

01:02:28.060 --> 01:02:31.870
I've often wondered,
Stephen Hawking--

01:02:31.870 --> 01:02:33.830
who I think was your
student, was he not?

01:02:33.830 --> 01:02:34.770
PENROSE: No, he was--

01:02:34.770 --> 01:02:35.620
WILSON: Well, that's what they--

01:02:35.620 --> 01:02:36.889
PENROSE: He wasn't my student.

01:02:36.889 --> 01:02:38.180
He was Dennis Sciama's student.

01:02:38.180 --> 01:02:40.096
WILSON: Let's put it you
lead the way for some

01:02:40.096 --> 01:02:41.750
of his own thinking.

01:02:41.750 --> 01:02:50.380
But he has once said that being
able to do kinesthetics now,

01:02:50.380 --> 01:02:54.040
he's glad he chose theoretical
physics, because it's

01:02:54.040 --> 01:02:55.480
all in my head.

01:02:55.480 --> 01:02:59.110
And I wondered, do
you have any sense

01:02:59.110 --> 01:03:07.660
of how not being able to
write, move, do these things

01:03:07.660 --> 01:03:12.970
but becoming purely
cerebral has maybe--

01:03:12.970 --> 01:03:16.090
maybe I've got it wrong,
but how this might influence

01:03:16.090 --> 01:03:17.860
his thinking or what
subjects he takes

01:03:17.860 --> 01:03:21.592
or anything else to comment?

01:03:21.592 --> 01:03:23.300
PENROSE: I mean, this
came on, of course,

01:03:23.300 --> 01:03:24.930
during the course
of his research.

01:03:24.930 --> 01:03:25.430
WILSON: Yes.

01:03:25.430 --> 01:03:28.820
He's had a long history of
being able to use his body.

01:03:28.820 --> 01:03:31.180
PENROSE: Well, he was
diagnosed, I think,

01:03:31.180 --> 01:03:35.817
in his first year of
research, graduate work.

01:03:35.817 --> 01:03:37.400
Dennis Sciama, who
was his supervisor,

01:03:37.400 --> 01:03:39.610
was told that he
probably wouldn't live

01:03:39.610 --> 01:03:42.430
the three years to do a PhD.

01:03:42.430 --> 01:03:44.860
Well, that was proved wrong.

01:03:44.860 --> 01:03:48.192
But I think Stephen
was very visual also.

01:03:48.192 --> 01:03:49.900
I mean, he was good
on the analytic side,

01:03:49.900 --> 01:03:53.540
but he was good on
the visual side.

01:03:53.540 --> 01:03:57.130
So presumably, earlier,
he was able to have

01:03:57.130 --> 01:04:00.230
a sort of feeling for things
in a tactile sense, as well.

01:04:00.230 --> 01:04:01.700
But I think the
visual aspect of it

01:04:01.700 --> 01:04:06.290
wouldn't have been
impaired by his condition.

01:04:06.290 --> 01:04:09.860
But later on, he would do
work by getting a student,

01:04:09.860 --> 01:04:12.530
and he would tell the student
what to put on the board.

01:04:12.530 --> 01:04:14.860
So that might be a
diagram sometimes,

01:04:14.860 --> 01:04:16.220
or it might be a calculation.

01:04:16.220 --> 01:04:18.035
WILSON: Does it by a
surrogate, and he sort of still

01:04:18.035 --> 01:04:18.850
does the visual.

01:04:18.850 --> 01:04:20.750
I mean, the movement
and the kinesthetic.

01:04:20.750 --> 01:04:22.833
PENROSE: That's right, but
the visual aspect of it

01:04:22.833 --> 01:04:26.840
was certainly very important
in a lot of his thinking.

01:04:26.840 --> 01:04:30.040
So I don't think that would have
been impaired by his condition.

01:04:30.040 --> 01:04:32.630
The kinesthetic aspect,
yes, I can see that.

01:04:32.630 --> 01:04:33.680
That must have been--

01:04:33.680 --> 01:04:36.790
LIGHTMAN: Why don't
we, at this point,

01:04:36.790 --> 01:04:42.400
open up the floor for
questions or comments?

01:04:42.400 --> 01:04:46.460
And I think we may have
some microphones here.

01:04:46.460 --> 01:04:50.520
So if you have a
question or a comment,

01:04:50.520 --> 01:04:55.030
please come down
to the microphone.

01:04:55.030 --> 01:04:58.790
And we'll just start right here.

01:04:58.790 --> 01:04:59.740
AUDIENCE: Hi.

01:04:59.740 --> 01:05:00.500
Can you hear me?

01:05:00.500 --> 01:05:02.500
OK.

01:05:02.500 --> 01:05:04.990
I was interested in
Ms. Sontag's remarks,

01:05:04.990 --> 01:05:07.390
and I sort of thought you
were going in a direction

01:05:07.390 --> 01:05:08.890
that you didn't quite get to.

01:05:12.820 --> 01:05:16.150
When you talk about these
as celebrity photographs,

01:05:16.150 --> 01:05:18.475
I think the reason that
they're celebrity photographs

01:05:18.475 --> 01:05:24.150
is because I think
they're so compelling.

01:05:24.150 --> 01:05:27.030
And maybe that just makes me
as naive as John Q. Public.

01:05:29.890 --> 01:05:33.380
I recognize all these, too,
but as I was watching them,

01:05:33.380 --> 01:05:35.440
I was sort of saying, wow.

01:05:35.440 --> 01:05:38.410
And what I'm struck by
really is the potential

01:05:38.410 --> 01:05:42.180
for photographs like
these to lie, basically,

01:05:42.180 --> 01:05:45.590
and to serve a primary
propaganda function.

01:05:45.590 --> 01:05:49.060
So, for instance,
if you look at this,

01:05:49.060 --> 01:05:52.610
what's remarkable about
this picture of the Earth,

01:05:52.610 --> 01:05:57.290
of the Earth rising, is
that when you look at this,

01:05:57.290 --> 01:06:00.430
you're suddenly struck by the
fact that 6 billion of us,

01:06:00.430 --> 01:06:04.000
most days, see the
moon in the sky.

01:06:04.000 --> 01:06:06.900
And none of us see it
the other way around.

01:06:06.900 --> 01:06:09.890
And this picture here
shows us the opposite,

01:06:09.890 --> 01:06:12.850
which is so out
of our experience

01:06:12.850 --> 01:06:16.880
and yet taken, I
believe, by human beings.

01:06:16.880 --> 01:06:20.350
That shows there's
been a tremendous shift

01:06:20.350 --> 01:06:23.080
in our perspective
of the universe.

01:06:23.080 --> 01:06:26.650
And frankly, I think it's
a wonderful add for NASA.

01:06:26.650 --> 01:06:28.690
Because you look at this
and you say, my god.

01:06:28.690 --> 01:06:31.030
You know, how can we not
give billions of dollars

01:06:31.030 --> 01:06:39.620
to those people so we can
change our view of the universe?

01:06:39.620 --> 01:06:42.100
This picture here,
Watson and Crick, it's

01:06:42.100 --> 01:06:44.620
not a photograph
taken by Mrs. Watson.

01:06:44.620 --> 01:06:47.910
It's not a snapshot
taken by Mrs. Watson.

01:06:47.910 --> 01:06:53.990
It's a marvelous picture
with a classic composition.

01:06:53.990 --> 01:06:56.980
You talked about the
framing of these,

01:06:56.980 --> 01:07:00.400
but it's really the
composition of this picture,

01:07:00.400 --> 01:07:02.445
the photograph, is what
makes it so remarkable.

01:07:02.445 --> 01:07:03.195
It's very classic.

01:07:06.040 --> 01:07:09.670
And Crick, the
movement of his body

01:07:09.670 --> 01:07:14.185
is mirroring the movement
of the double helix here.

01:07:16.870 --> 01:07:19.760
And what's so striking about
this is we've all seen,

01:07:19.760 --> 01:07:24.100
probably, pictures of Goddard
standing next to his rocket.

01:07:24.100 --> 01:07:27.340
Well, Goddard is all
dressed up in a suit.

01:07:27.340 --> 01:07:28.690
His tie is very neat.

01:07:28.690 --> 01:07:30.080
Everything's in place.

01:07:30.080 --> 01:07:32.099
And he looks,
frankly, like a nerd.

01:07:32.099 --> 01:07:34.390
Everybody's seen these pictures
from the '30s, I think.

01:07:34.390 --> 01:07:36.320
There was recently
another one in the--

01:07:36.320 --> 01:07:38.740
whereas, there's
something very fetching

01:07:38.740 --> 01:07:41.170
about Watson and Crick.

01:07:41.170 --> 01:07:43.810
Watson, I think that's
Watson on the left--

01:07:43.810 --> 01:07:45.340
LIGHTMAN: I'm going to--

01:07:45.340 --> 01:07:47.440
because we have a lot of
people wanting questions.

01:07:47.440 --> 01:07:48.486
I'm sorry to--

01:07:48.486 --> 01:07:49.610
AUDIENCE: Let me just one--

01:07:49.610 --> 01:07:52.174
if you look at that
picture of the fetus--

01:07:52.174 --> 01:07:55.126
[APPLAUSE]

01:07:56.660 --> 01:07:58.890
LIGHTMAN: Let's
take one over here.

01:07:58.890 --> 01:08:00.844
AUDIENCE: I hope I
don't go on that long.

01:08:03.980 --> 01:08:07.310
I see this panel a little
bit as my daily life,

01:08:07.310 --> 01:08:10.670
because we've got the scientists
on the right and the writers

01:08:10.670 --> 01:08:11.210
on the left.

01:08:11.210 --> 01:08:14.280
And I'm in the center,
because my job, for example,

01:08:14.280 --> 01:08:16.042
coming to Science
magazine was to turn it

01:08:16.042 --> 01:08:18.500
from what I'm going to show
you tomorrow, which some of you

01:08:18.500 --> 01:08:21.229
remember, which was a totally
black and white-- had almost no

01:08:21.229 --> 01:08:25.500
photos, no illustrations-- to
something you recognize today,

01:08:25.500 --> 01:08:29.310
which has images for the purpose
that Susan worries about,

01:08:29.310 --> 01:08:32.450
which is to try to make
our audience broader.

01:08:32.450 --> 01:08:35.450
When I came, the audience
was getting older,

01:08:35.450 --> 01:08:38.870
and young people were used to
a colorful Newsweek and Time

01:08:38.870 --> 01:08:40.319
kind of universe.

01:08:40.319 --> 01:08:43.310
And we knew that we weren't
going to have subscribers

01:08:43.310 --> 01:08:45.950
if we stayed sort of like the
New England Journal of Medicine

01:08:45.950 --> 01:08:47.720
is with its cover.

01:08:47.720 --> 01:08:51.680
As you know, it just has tables
of contents on the cover.

01:08:51.680 --> 01:08:56.390
So I think we live with this
interesting battle in our staff

01:08:56.390 --> 01:08:56.990
all the time.

01:08:56.990 --> 01:08:59.531
How much space should we give
to images versus how much space

01:08:59.531 --> 01:09:01.100
do we need for our words?

01:09:01.100 --> 01:09:04.140
And I thought it'd be just fun
to tell one story for the panel

01:09:04.140 --> 01:09:06.920
and see if it relates
to this sort of thinking

01:09:06.920 --> 01:09:08.210
that we have here.

01:09:08.210 --> 01:09:12.470
I had one time come
to Newsweek, and one

01:09:12.470 --> 01:09:14.240
of my first and most
important lessons

01:09:14.240 --> 01:09:17.210
when I started working in
Newsweek was the following.

01:09:17.210 --> 01:09:20.660
It was right at the second
most lively period--

01:09:20.660 --> 01:09:23.990
the most lively period after
Watergate, when the Iran Contra

01:09:23.990 --> 01:09:26.080
events were taking place.

01:09:26.080 --> 01:09:31.069
And I learned what made a great
writer for Newsweek or Time

01:09:31.069 --> 01:09:32.420
by the following.

01:09:32.420 --> 01:09:35.000
I would meet people
in some months

01:09:35.000 --> 01:09:37.939
after the first press conference
Reagan had-- you'll remember,

01:09:37.939 --> 01:09:39.170
some of you.

01:09:39.170 --> 01:09:41.569
After many months, when there
were no press conferences

01:09:41.569 --> 01:09:44.242
because he was hiding,
worrying about what to say.

01:09:44.242 --> 01:09:46.700
And I would meet people, and
they'd say, where do you work?

01:09:46.700 --> 01:09:47.616
And I'd say, Newsweek.

01:09:47.616 --> 01:09:50.899
And they'd say, oh, you know,
I really loved that story

01:09:50.899 --> 01:09:55.190
that you guys did where that
guy wrote that sentence,

01:09:55.190 --> 01:09:57.050
Ronald Reagan is
a tall man, but he

01:09:57.050 --> 01:09:59.480
looked small behind
the podium last night

01:09:59.480 --> 01:10:02.030
when he tried to explain
what was going on.

01:10:02.030 --> 01:10:04.010
And I call that a word picture.

01:10:04.010 --> 01:10:06.080
And the word picture
has the same function

01:10:06.080 --> 01:10:08.340
as the images when
they're working right,

01:10:08.340 --> 01:10:10.760
which is to be able
to teach people

01:10:10.760 --> 01:10:12.780
a lesson in a very quick way.

01:10:12.780 --> 01:10:15.560
And I think the contrast
between that important kind

01:10:15.560 --> 01:10:18.350
of iconic way of doing
word pictures or image

01:10:18.350 --> 01:10:20.406
pictures versus
the kind of thing

01:10:20.406 --> 01:10:22.280
that Susan Sontag probably
is worrying about,

01:10:22.280 --> 01:10:26.540
like the sort of Mao image,
or even the word image

01:10:26.540 --> 01:10:31.080
that is like Mao propaganda
that entire populations stop

01:10:31.080 --> 01:10:33.080
thinking about what's
behind them because that's

01:10:33.080 --> 01:10:36.110
the image, to me, that's the
interesting debate that's

01:10:36.110 --> 01:10:37.820
going on there in the panel.

01:10:37.820 --> 01:10:39.420
And that's what I
wanted to mention.

01:10:39.420 --> 01:10:39.920
Thanks.

01:10:39.920 --> 01:10:41.170
LIGHTMAN: Thank you very much.

01:10:41.170 --> 01:10:42.260
Thank you.

01:10:42.260 --> 01:10:44.120
Let's go here.

01:10:44.120 --> 01:10:44.800
AUDIENCE: Hi.

01:10:44.800 --> 01:10:48.200
I think that the images have
been misclassified, actually.

01:10:48.200 --> 01:10:51.290
The bomb and the baby,
yes, they're images.

01:10:51.290 --> 01:10:53.810
I think Watson and Crick
are illustrating a model.

01:10:53.810 --> 01:10:57.570
And I also think that the
human evolution, that's

01:10:57.570 --> 01:10:58.470
illustrating a model.

01:10:58.470 --> 01:11:01.790
In other words, those two
images are getting across ideas.

01:11:01.790 --> 01:11:07.940
And then, the moon and the
picture of the brain, that's

01:11:07.940 --> 01:11:09.350
actually data.

01:11:09.350 --> 01:11:15.080
So those observations we
then infer results from.

01:11:15.080 --> 01:11:18.110
So I think you can too easily
dismiss all these images as

01:11:18.110 --> 01:11:19.827
merely images.

01:11:19.827 --> 01:11:20.660
LIGHTMAN: Thank you.

01:11:20.660 --> 01:11:22.035
SONTAG: Can I just
say something?

01:11:22.035 --> 01:11:25.260
I'm not trying to
dismiss these images.

01:11:25.260 --> 01:11:28.700
I'm saying that there
is a problem in thinking

01:11:28.700 --> 01:11:31.530
that images really speak to us.

01:11:31.530 --> 01:11:34.162
I think it's more that
we're speaking to them.

01:11:34.162 --> 01:11:35.120
That's what I'm saying.

01:11:35.120 --> 01:11:37.160
Of course, these are
wonderful images.

01:11:37.160 --> 01:11:40.070
One of my most
treasured books, if I

01:11:40.070 --> 01:11:46.100
had to sort of pare my
inflated private library down

01:11:46.100 --> 01:11:49.750
to a few hundred books,
I'm sure the NASA--

01:11:49.750 --> 01:11:53.060
I have a big, big book
of NASA photographs

01:11:53.060 --> 01:11:55.520
of things seen from the moon.

01:11:55.520 --> 01:11:56.780
Includes that image.

01:11:56.780 --> 01:11:57.625
I adore that image.

01:11:57.625 --> 01:11:59.000
It's not that I
don't think these

01:11:59.000 --> 01:12:01.330
are great images, most of them.

01:12:01.330 --> 01:12:05.550
I just think there's a
problem in our using images

01:12:05.550 --> 01:12:09.020
iconically, that they're
not really telling us.

01:12:09.020 --> 01:12:10.430
We're telling them.

01:12:10.430 --> 01:12:14.600
We're using them as
platforms for associations.

01:12:14.600 --> 01:12:16.730
And that we have to
understand always that images

01:12:16.730 --> 01:12:18.440
are seen in a context.

01:12:18.440 --> 01:12:21.290
I'm not saying these aren't
good images or beautiful images

01:12:21.290 --> 01:12:23.030
or exciting images.

01:12:23.030 --> 01:12:26.000
And I'm not, certainly,
saying that they're not

01:12:26.000 --> 01:12:27.240
famous for a reason.

01:12:27.240 --> 01:12:29.365
They're famous for a reason
because several of them

01:12:29.365 --> 01:12:30.680
are absolutely wonderful.

01:12:30.680 --> 01:12:33.200
The question is, what
do they actually say?

01:12:33.200 --> 01:12:34.830
Do they speak for themselves?

01:12:34.830 --> 01:12:37.290
I think they speak very
little for themselves,

01:12:37.290 --> 01:12:38.930
including Crick and Watson.

01:12:38.930 --> 01:12:41.000
I don't think that's
telling you anything.

01:12:41.000 --> 01:12:44.390
It's because we care about them
that we care about that image.

01:12:44.390 --> 01:12:47.750
PENROSE: But wasn't the point of
them to stimulate conversation?

01:12:47.750 --> 01:12:49.250
I thought that was the idea.

01:12:49.250 --> 01:12:51.260
SONTAG: Well, it is, but
that's the conversation

01:12:51.260 --> 01:12:52.670
that it stimulated in me.

01:12:52.670 --> 01:12:54.644
It stimulated in me--

01:12:54.644 --> 01:12:57.512
[APPLAUSE]

01:12:58.470 --> 01:13:01.910
--the conversation that
there's a problem about dealing

01:13:01.910 --> 01:13:05.090
with very well-known
images that we can

01:13:05.090 --> 01:13:07.700
simply associate to
as opposed to images

01:13:07.700 --> 01:13:11.540
that are more obscure or
more grouped, I think.

01:13:11.540 --> 01:13:15.680
Grouped in the
sense of sequential.

01:13:15.680 --> 01:13:16.910
AUDIENCE: Hi.

01:13:16.910 --> 01:13:18.770
Susan Sontag, I
enjoyed your comment

01:13:18.770 --> 01:13:21.800
that these celebrity images
are the visual equivalent

01:13:21.800 --> 01:13:23.750
of sound bites.

01:13:23.750 --> 01:13:26.300
Thanks very much for
that memorable comment.

01:13:26.300 --> 01:13:29.990
You also said, though, that
you worried that we're not

01:13:29.990 --> 01:13:32.750
learning anything from
the images beyond what we

01:13:32.750 --> 01:13:35.840
would from reading
narrative and text.

01:13:35.840 --> 01:13:37.640
And you also implied--

01:13:37.640 --> 01:13:39.380
you asked whether
images are actually

01:13:39.380 --> 01:13:41.600
helping democratization,
even implying

01:13:41.600 --> 01:13:44.330
that there's some sort
of tyranny of an image.

01:13:44.330 --> 01:13:46.420
And I want to give
you a different image,

01:13:46.420 --> 01:13:51.110
a different picture, of
how images aren't tyranny.

01:13:51.110 --> 01:13:53.450
When I read a scientific
journal article

01:13:53.450 --> 01:13:57.710
in my area of expertise, I
begin reading the article,

01:13:57.710 --> 01:14:01.430
and as soon as I've
gotten the main idea,

01:14:01.430 --> 01:14:03.920
I stop reading the
text and I turn

01:14:03.920 --> 01:14:07.940
to whatever non-text material
the author has given me.

01:14:07.940 --> 01:14:11.450
I turn to the tables,
the charts, the pictures,

01:14:11.450 --> 01:14:14.750
perhaps the PET scans if
it's a neuroscience article.

01:14:14.750 --> 01:14:17.480
And at that point, I'm not
reading the text anymore,

01:14:17.480 --> 01:14:20.450
and I'm reading the images.

01:14:20.450 --> 01:14:22.730
And anyone else do that?

01:14:26.300 --> 01:14:29.330
I think I'm doing it, and I'd
like to get your reaction,

01:14:29.330 --> 01:14:33.150
because I want the pleasure
and achievement of figuring out

01:14:33.150 --> 01:14:37.700
the story for myself and coming
to my own interpretation.

01:14:37.700 --> 01:14:38.890
Thank you.

01:14:38.890 --> 01:14:41.204
LIGHTMAN: Thank you.

01:14:41.204 --> 01:14:42.890
[APPLAUSE]

01:14:44.320 --> 01:14:47.840
SONTAG: I guess I don't know,
really, how to respond to that.

01:14:47.840 --> 01:14:50.390
First of all, I never
used the word tyranny

01:14:50.390 --> 01:14:51.740
or any of those notions.

01:14:51.740 --> 01:14:53.690
That's very much not
what I was saying.

01:14:53.690 --> 01:14:57.050
But I guess I'm not so
interested in figuring things

01:14:57.050 --> 01:14:57.860
out for myself.

01:14:57.860 --> 01:14:59.730
I'm more interested in learning.

01:14:59.730 --> 01:15:01.680
So I wouldn't say that
I would abandon a text

01:15:01.680 --> 01:15:05.240
to look at the image, as
much as I am extremely visual

01:15:05.240 --> 01:15:08.560
and spend a great deal of
time looking at images.

01:15:08.560 --> 01:15:11.300
I mean, Alan Lightman
picked me up at the airport,

01:15:11.300 --> 01:15:14.180
and we went to the museum
and looked at images

01:15:14.180 --> 01:15:15.102
at the Boston Museum.

01:15:15.102 --> 01:15:16.310
You have a great museum here.

01:15:16.310 --> 01:15:19.400
I don't get to Boston more
than every two months or so,

01:15:19.400 --> 01:15:20.640
and I go to a museum.

01:15:20.640 --> 01:15:22.110
I live in a visual world.

01:15:22.110 --> 01:15:25.040
I'm visually besotted.

01:15:25.040 --> 01:15:28.550
I'm just saying I think
the question of what

01:15:28.550 --> 01:15:32.500
we learn from images and what
kinds of images we learn from

01:15:32.500 --> 01:15:37.770
and the very many different
contexts that we look at images

01:15:37.770 --> 01:15:40.801
in is a critical question.

01:15:40.801 --> 01:15:41.300
That's all.

01:15:41.300 --> 01:15:44.374
I'm not talking about
images tyrannize over us

01:15:44.374 --> 01:15:45.290
or anything like that.

01:15:45.290 --> 01:15:47.571
There's so many different
uses for images.

01:15:47.571 --> 01:15:51.150
WILSON: May I suggest that
much of what is being said

01:15:51.150 --> 01:15:53.070
is missing the point.

01:15:53.070 --> 01:15:58.320
If the aim is to somehow conjoin
science and the humanities,

01:15:58.320 --> 01:16:01.015
the best of science and
the best of humanities--

01:16:01.015 --> 01:16:03.260
in other words, what
you've been describing

01:16:03.260 --> 01:16:05.200
is a kind of phenomenon.

01:16:05.200 --> 01:16:06.680
You don't repeat
it over and over

01:16:06.680 --> 01:16:10.010
again without the framework
and relevance and so on.

01:16:10.010 --> 01:16:14.250
The question of interest,
a question of interest,

01:16:14.250 --> 01:16:17.380
is, why do we respond this way?

01:16:17.380 --> 01:16:20.970
Why do certain images
affect us a certain way?

01:16:20.970 --> 01:16:25.110
Why does framing occur
at a certain degree?

01:16:25.110 --> 01:16:28.100
Why do we select a certain
complexity of imagery

01:16:28.100 --> 01:16:30.520
in art and photography?

01:16:30.520 --> 01:16:33.360
If we can't answer
why, then we really

01:16:33.360 --> 01:16:36.410
don't understand the
human condition very well.

01:16:36.410 --> 01:16:40.290
And I suggest that where
science and humanities are

01:16:40.290 --> 01:16:43.560
likely to come together
in this particular subject

01:16:43.560 --> 01:16:46.280
is, in fact,
through neuroscience

01:16:46.280 --> 01:16:49.720
and through the reconstruction
of human evolutionary history

01:16:49.720 --> 01:16:52.140
pieced together with
the particularities

01:16:52.140 --> 01:16:53.580
of cultural history.

01:16:53.580 --> 01:16:57.270
And that then, knowing how
the brain is constructed

01:16:57.270 --> 01:17:00.750
and programmed, what
our sensory biases are,

01:17:00.750 --> 01:17:05.130
how we develop these
capacities and biases

01:17:05.130 --> 01:17:09.500
and emotional responses, then
we will have a true theory

01:17:09.500 --> 01:17:11.457
of, say, the arts.

01:17:11.457 --> 01:17:13.040
LIGHTMAN: I noticed
from the audience,

01:17:13.040 --> 01:17:16.030
we've been getting a lot
more comments than questions,

01:17:16.030 --> 01:17:19.150
which is just fine.

01:17:19.150 --> 01:17:20.440
Let's go here.

01:17:20.440 --> 01:17:24.280
AUDIENCE: I believe
that vision and language

01:17:24.280 --> 01:17:26.980
and abstract thinking
have common origins

01:17:26.980 --> 01:17:33.100
in the physiology of vision
and thinking and language.

01:17:33.100 --> 01:17:35.670
So the process of getting
meaning from image

01:17:35.670 --> 01:17:39.940
through paying attention
and parsing and interpreting

01:17:39.940 --> 01:17:43.450
and ultimately changing
our internal state

01:17:43.450 --> 01:17:46.990
is substantially
similar to the process

01:17:46.990 --> 01:17:51.340
of understanding a speech act
or understanding this thought

01:17:51.340 --> 01:17:52.360
process.

01:17:52.360 --> 01:17:56.330
So could you comment on that?

01:17:56.330 --> 01:17:56.910
SONTAG: Yeah.

01:17:56.910 --> 01:18:00.250
I certainly think--
as I said earlier,

01:18:00.250 --> 01:18:03.360
I don't think there's a
fundamental difference.

01:18:03.360 --> 01:18:07.540
I mean, the title of this
meeting is image and meaning.

01:18:07.540 --> 01:18:13.690
The meaning is something which
is internal, if you'd like.

01:18:13.690 --> 01:18:16.570
But it's not so different
from how one responds

01:18:16.570 --> 01:18:22.020
to verbal or written words.

01:18:22.020 --> 01:18:23.860
And again, there's
something down there.

01:18:23.860 --> 01:18:26.470
There's some configuration
of words on the page,

01:18:26.470 --> 01:18:31.540
and the meaning comes in
you when you read that.

01:18:31.540 --> 01:18:33.070
And it's similar to an image.

01:18:33.070 --> 01:18:35.620
I mean, the image may
convey some meaning to you,

01:18:35.620 --> 01:18:39.940
which it isn't there, in a
sense, abstractly in the image.

01:18:39.940 --> 01:18:44.500
It's something which is how
one responds to the image.

01:18:44.500 --> 01:18:46.960
All I'm saying is I think I'm
agreeing with you, if that's

01:18:46.960 --> 01:18:50.860
what you're saying, that there
isn't a fundamental difference

01:18:50.860 --> 01:18:53.410
between how one responds
to a visual image

01:18:53.410 --> 01:18:55.630
and how one responds
to words, say.

01:18:55.630 --> 01:18:59.150
It's just different part
of the brain, for example,

01:18:59.150 --> 01:19:01.930
and it has different
characteristics,

01:19:01.930 --> 01:19:05.644
valuable in slightly
different ways.

01:19:05.644 --> 01:19:07.060
AUDIENCE: And the
meaning would be

01:19:07.060 --> 01:19:10.580
that we change the
distribution of propensities

01:19:10.580 --> 01:19:13.080
to do things in the future.

01:19:13.080 --> 01:19:15.910
PENROSE: Meaning's one of
these very difficult things

01:19:15.910 --> 01:19:17.350
to come to terms with.

01:19:17.350 --> 01:19:20.050
And I think it does
require consciousness.

01:19:20.050 --> 01:19:22.390
I mean, something
can't mean anything

01:19:22.390 --> 01:19:25.270
without being aware
of it, it seems to me.

01:19:25.270 --> 01:19:27.880
I mean, that's why it's
a very difficult issue,

01:19:27.880 --> 01:19:30.580
because it's things
that we don't

01:19:30.580 --> 01:19:32.560
know much about at the moment.

01:19:32.560 --> 01:19:34.930
I mean, my experience
comes largely

01:19:34.930 --> 01:19:36.850
from things like
mathematics, where

01:19:36.850 --> 01:19:40.990
one can see that it's
fundamentally important what

01:19:40.990 --> 01:19:43.720
the meaning is behind
a set of symbols.

01:19:43.720 --> 01:19:50.020
I mean, you can't
know whether something

01:19:50.020 --> 01:19:53.650
is right or wrong, if you like,
without knowing what it means.

01:19:53.650 --> 01:19:56.650
Just simply manipulating the
symbols isn't sufficient.

01:19:56.650 --> 01:19:58.860
The meaning that
underlies those symbols

01:19:58.860 --> 01:20:01.270
is often very important.

01:20:01.270 --> 01:20:03.490
Sometimes you can get
away without the meaning,

01:20:03.490 --> 01:20:06.500
but ultimately, you need to
know what these things mean.

01:20:06.500 --> 01:20:08.656
And it must apply,
also, to visual images.

01:20:08.656 --> 01:20:10.030
AUDIENCE: But
subliminal messages

01:20:10.030 --> 01:20:13.350
may convey meaning
without awareness.

01:20:13.350 --> 01:20:15.560
PENROSE: Well, I don't think so.

01:20:15.560 --> 01:20:17.680
No, they could make
you do things, maybe,

01:20:17.680 --> 01:20:19.030
or they could make you--

01:20:19.030 --> 01:20:20.980
but I don't think
the meaning is there.

01:20:20.980 --> 01:20:26.350
I think that's more a kind
of automatic response,

01:20:26.350 --> 01:20:26.950
if they work.

01:20:26.950 --> 01:20:28.700
I'm never quite sure
whether they do work.

01:20:28.700 --> 01:20:31.790
But if they do, it's, as you
say, an unconscious thing.

01:20:31.790 --> 01:20:33.187
That's the whole point.

01:20:33.187 --> 01:20:35.020
But it's not really
there's any meaning that

01:20:35.020 --> 01:20:35.853
gets through to you.

01:20:35.853 --> 01:20:40.300
It's that you may feel
you like something

01:20:40.300 --> 01:20:42.070
that you didn't like otherwise.

01:20:42.070 --> 01:20:43.753
Maybe that's how it works.

01:20:43.753 --> 01:20:47.434
But I don't see any actual
meaning there, really.

01:20:47.434 --> 01:20:48.850
LIGHTMAN: Let's
move to a question

01:20:48.850 --> 01:20:50.020
or a comment over here.

01:20:50.020 --> 01:20:51.800
AUDIENCE: Yes,
thank you very much.

01:20:51.800 --> 01:20:53.140
My name is Michael Charney.

01:20:53.140 --> 01:20:55.810
I want to thank the panel
for allowing the audience

01:20:55.810 --> 01:20:58.066
to participate in this
wonderful dialogue.

01:20:58.066 --> 01:20:59.440
I'd like to say
that, by the way,

01:20:59.440 --> 01:21:01.660
sight bite is
another way to talk

01:21:01.660 --> 01:21:04.000
about a sound bite for the eye.

01:21:04.000 --> 01:21:07.300
Perhaps it's B-Y-T-E
in the current context.

01:21:07.300 --> 01:21:10.216
In any event, looking at
this, I see an ensemble.

01:21:10.216 --> 01:21:11.590
Perhaps they're
icons and they're

01:21:11.590 --> 01:21:14.140
trivial or trite cliches,
but actually, there's

01:21:14.140 --> 01:21:15.160
a narrative here.

01:21:15.160 --> 01:21:17.110
And in looking at
these images, for me,

01:21:17.110 --> 01:21:19.530
I try to see what's in
the mind of the person who

01:21:19.530 --> 01:21:21.280
edited and selected
them and arranged them

01:21:21.280 --> 01:21:22.840
in a particular order.

01:21:22.840 --> 01:21:25.410
What I see here is
seeing the unseen.

01:21:25.410 --> 01:21:28.660
And so image and meaning, the
image is giving a meaning.

01:21:28.660 --> 01:21:31.570
The meaning is what do we
see, what do we want to see?

01:21:31.570 --> 01:21:33.490
Here, we see, I think,
my interpretation,

01:21:33.490 --> 01:21:36.940
is a creation myth, as
done perhaps for MIT.

01:21:36.940 --> 01:21:38.490
It is rather cold
in its own way.

01:21:41.425 --> 01:21:44.080
We start out with energy.

01:21:44.080 --> 01:21:45.870
Perhaps God is up
there in the sky,

01:21:45.870 --> 01:21:48.480
but there is this
light and dark,

01:21:48.480 --> 01:21:49.960
a creation of light and dark.

01:21:49.960 --> 01:21:51.130
The Earth is then separated.

01:21:51.130 --> 01:21:52.900
We see the Earth, the water.

01:21:52.900 --> 01:21:53.710
It's been created.

01:21:53.710 --> 01:21:56.200
We then see the
beginning of life

01:21:56.200 --> 01:22:00.100
in the image of the
Watson-Crick genome.

01:22:00.100 --> 01:22:01.810
We then see the
product of that, which

01:22:01.810 --> 01:22:06.580
is the evolutionary
stage of primates.

01:22:06.580 --> 01:22:12.820
Notice that we're missing plants
and other general taxonomic

01:22:12.820 --> 01:22:13.910
groups.

01:22:13.910 --> 01:22:19.821
Then we see the neonate, which
is recapitulating phylogeny,

01:22:19.821 --> 01:22:20.320
I guess.

01:22:23.420 --> 01:22:25.350
And then we see consciousness.

01:22:25.350 --> 01:22:28.480
We see an image of creation
and the creative thought,

01:22:28.480 --> 01:22:29.860
which then closes the circle.

01:22:29.860 --> 01:22:32.800
Because then we're back to
the mind of the creator.

01:22:32.800 --> 01:22:35.058
Thank you.

01:22:35.058 --> 01:22:37.950
[APPLAUSE]

01:22:40.842 --> 01:22:43.910
SONTAG: I do think it takes
quite a feat of imagination

01:22:43.910 --> 01:22:48.680
to see that first image has an
image of energy and creation,

01:22:48.680 --> 01:22:50.796
but maybe I'm being
too literal here.

01:22:50.796 --> 01:22:51.790
AUDIENCE: Big bang.

01:22:51.790 --> 01:22:53.820
WILSON: It's the little bang.

01:22:53.820 --> 01:22:55.430
SONTAG: Looks like
an atom bomb to me,

01:22:55.430 --> 01:22:58.394
but I'm just being
naive, I guess.

01:22:58.394 --> 01:23:00.060
LIGHTMAN: That was a
brilliant analysis.

01:23:00.060 --> 01:23:02.705
PENROSE: Was that
the idea, or did he--

01:23:02.705 --> 01:23:04.440
WILSON: Confess.

01:23:04.440 --> 01:23:06.327
LIGHTMAN: It was not
the conscious idea.

01:23:06.327 --> 01:23:08.660
But since we're talking about
the unconscious as well as

01:23:08.660 --> 01:23:11.464
the consciousness, who knows?

01:23:11.464 --> 01:23:12.380
Another question here.

01:23:12.380 --> 01:23:12.710
AUDIENCE: Hi.

01:23:12.710 --> 01:23:13.251
Good evening.

01:23:13.251 --> 01:23:15.660
I was wondering if each of
you, and if it's appropriate,

01:23:15.660 --> 01:23:17.090
Mr. Lightman, you
as well, comment

01:23:17.090 --> 01:23:21.860
on the distinction between
science communication and art,

01:23:21.860 --> 01:23:23.674
as concisely as
possible, if you can.

01:23:23.674 --> 01:23:24.590
WILSON: May I do that?

01:23:27.992 --> 01:23:31.580
May I give that a try, Alan?

01:23:31.580 --> 01:23:33.070
Let me give that a try.

01:23:33.070 --> 01:23:35.830
Science communication
as you would

01:23:35.830 --> 01:23:36.970
get in science and nature.

01:23:36.970 --> 01:23:38.620
I'm trying to be brief.

01:23:38.620 --> 01:23:43.170
It's all about
objectivity repeatability.

01:23:43.170 --> 01:23:46.030
It is not about metaphor.

01:23:46.030 --> 01:23:48.460
Metaphor is not welcome.

01:23:48.460 --> 01:23:50.770
Emotional expression
is not welcome,

01:23:50.770 --> 01:23:53.830
unless it's done very
chastely in the introduction

01:23:53.830 --> 01:23:55.870
of the discussion.

01:23:55.870 --> 01:23:59.380
The exact reverse of--

01:23:59.380 --> 01:24:01.180
and it's about repeatability.

01:24:01.180 --> 01:24:03.250
The exact reverse
is true of art.

01:24:03.250 --> 01:24:07.180
Art is designed to
transmit emotion directly

01:24:07.180 --> 01:24:11.200
from the creator
to the audience.

01:24:11.200 --> 01:24:14.590
And it is all about metaphor.

01:24:14.590 --> 01:24:17.170
The gold and silver
of science writing

01:24:17.170 --> 01:24:21.640
is the discovery of
something that is verifiably

01:24:21.640 --> 01:24:24.110
new in the physical world.

01:24:24.110 --> 01:24:30.790
The gold and silver of art is
the power and the originality

01:24:30.790 --> 01:24:34.270
of the metaphor and the
expression and the impact,

01:24:34.270 --> 01:24:37.576
the general nature of the
impact it has on the audience.

01:24:37.576 --> 01:24:40.474
[APPLAUSE]

01:24:43.154 --> 01:24:44.820
PENROSE: I'll just
make a brief comment.

01:24:44.820 --> 01:24:46.250
And I think they are different.

01:24:46.250 --> 01:24:52.360
And the purpose of science
journalism is clarity.

01:24:52.360 --> 01:24:54.920
You want to get an idea
across as clearly as you can,

01:24:54.920 --> 01:24:57.440
and you might use a
visual image to do this.

01:24:57.440 --> 01:25:01.470
Whereas, art isn't
necessarily trying to do that.

01:25:01.470 --> 01:25:03.550
But on the other
hand, I do think

01:25:03.550 --> 01:25:06.380
it is important that these
images should be pleasing.

01:25:06.380 --> 01:25:08.370
And to have an
artistic quality means

01:25:08.370 --> 01:25:10.400
you're more likely to
want to look at them.

01:25:10.400 --> 01:25:13.310
And I think it's
important, but it shouldn't

01:25:13.310 --> 01:25:14.690
detract from the clarity.

01:25:14.690 --> 01:25:17.720
I think the clarity is
the major thing that

01:25:17.720 --> 01:25:21.530
is important in science
journalism, at least

01:25:21.530 --> 01:25:24.110
the visual part of it.

01:25:24.110 --> 01:25:26.200
Clarity's important to
both parts, but I mean,

01:25:26.200 --> 01:25:28.790
we're talking about
the visual image.

01:25:28.790 --> 01:25:33.340
Whereas, art doesn't necessarily
aim at the same thing.

01:25:33.340 --> 01:25:38.790
But I would like to see
art also in such images.

01:25:38.790 --> 01:25:40.110
It's not the important thing.

01:25:40.110 --> 01:25:42.710
LIGHTMAN: Let's have a
comment or question here.

01:25:42.710 --> 01:25:45.460
AUDIENCE: I'd like to make a
comment on the Watson and Crick

01:25:45.460 --> 01:25:46.870
photo.

01:25:46.870 --> 01:25:50.690
This is a very iconic image,
and it represents, of course,

01:25:50.690 --> 01:25:54.990
one of the century's
greatest discoveries.

01:25:54.990 --> 01:25:57.610
And this has a bearing on
both this morning's session

01:25:57.610 --> 01:26:00.310
and on Susan Sontag's comments.

01:26:00.310 --> 01:26:02.760
I think this photo is
most interesting for what

01:26:02.760 --> 01:26:05.560
it actually leaves out,
and that's the fact

01:26:05.560 --> 01:26:08.980
that Watson and Crick
based their model, in part,

01:26:08.980 --> 01:26:12.535
on data which they took
from Rosalind Franklin.

01:26:12.535 --> 01:26:15.397
[APPLAUSE]

01:26:16.360 --> 01:26:19.710
And I think this is
image, which we see over

01:26:19.710 --> 01:26:23.500
and over again, it really
only tells us half the story.

01:26:23.500 --> 01:26:26.310
And I wondered if people
could comment on that.

01:26:26.310 --> 01:26:29.130
SONTAG: Well, we've got two
images that rather obscure

01:26:29.130 --> 01:26:32.590
half the human race, don't we?

01:26:32.590 --> 01:26:34.830
I think it's very
important, absolutely.

01:26:34.830 --> 01:26:39.030
They're iconic images
and images that tell us

01:26:39.030 --> 01:26:40.260
what we should remember.

01:26:40.260 --> 01:26:41.570
That's one of the
things that-- that's

01:26:41.570 --> 01:26:43.528
what I started to say at
the end of my remarks,

01:26:43.528 --> 01:26:45.300
rather incoherently,
that images tell us

01:26:45.300 --> 01:26:47.130
what we should remember.

01:26:47.130 --> 01:26:49.800
And they occlude
or obscure things,

01:26:49.800 --> 01:26:51.060
cast them into darkness.

01:26:51.060 --> 01:26:52.950
And that's a perfect example.

01:26:52.950 --> 01:26:54.450
I'm very glad you
made that point.

01:26:57.312 --> 01:26:58.270
LIGHTMAN: Comment here.

01:26:58.270 --> 01:26:59.850
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

01:26:59.850 --> 01:27:01.620
I'm sure we all
bring our own context

01:27:01.620 --> 01:27:02.970
to what we're seeing up here.

01:27:02.970 --> 01:27:07.200
And I have a brief comment
and a quick question.

01:27:07.200 --> 01:27:12.690
And when I looked at the images,
it seems to me that all of them

01:27:12.690 --> 01:27:16.470
can cause us to reflect on a
number of different things.

01:27:16.470 --> 01:27:20.190
But that brain scan, I'm
not a neuroscientist,

01:27:20.190 --> 01:27:23.490
but it may be the state in
which the mind is actually

01:27:23.490 --> 01:27:25.020
meditative in reflection.

01:27:25.020 --> 01:27:26.970
There's been some
articles of that of late

01:27:26.970 --> 01:27:30.150
in some of the
magazines and journals.

01:27:30.150 --> 01:27:32.820
When I see the
images together, all

01:27:32.820 --> 01:27:34.590
of them except
the upper far left

01:27:34.590 --> 01:27:37.380
have to do with
life or evolution

01:27:37.380 --> 01:27:40.590
or reflecting on that
beautiful planet or that child

01:27:40.590 --> 01:27:43.410
or that fetus or
that reflective mind

01:27:43.410 --> 01:27:47.630
or discovering life in DNA,
except the one on the left.

01:27:47.630 --> 01:27:48.630
I agree with you, Susan.

01:27:48.630 --> 01:27:52.920
To me, I see destruction, so
that was quite a contrast.

01:27:52.920 --> 01:27:55.110
I'm wondering if you could
share with us briefly,

01:27:55.110 --> 01:28:00.390
if you had to describe your
image of what would represent

01:28:00.390 --> 01:28:03.810
God is, what would that visual
image be for each of you,

01:28:03.810 --> 01:28:05.381
if you could share that?

01:28:05.381 --> 01:28:05.880
Thank you.

01:28:11.990 --> 01:28:14.360
PENROSE: I actually drew
a picture of a creator

01:28:14.360 --> 01:28:15.710
in one of my books.

01:28:15.710 --> 01:28:17.330
And Susan might be
interested in this,

01:28:17.330 --> 01:28:21.260
because I gave a lecture in
which I'd shown this picture.

01:28:21.260 --> 01:28:24.110
And somebody, at the end of
the lecture, asked the question

01:28:24.110 --> 01:28:26.720
and said, in your
depiction of God

01:28:26.720 --> 01:28:31.967
creating the universe, why did
you depict her with a beard?

01:28:31.967 --> 01:28:34.550
And so I said, well, look, let's
have that picture back again.

01:28:34.550 --> 01:28:36.258
And I pointed out
that, actually, there's

01:28:36.258 --> 01:28:38.060
deliberately two
different interpretations

01:28:38.060 --> 01:28:39.020
of that picture.

01:28:39.020 --> 01:28:41.611
One is a beard, and the other
is the hair coming around

01:28:41.611 --> 01:28:42.110
like this.

01:28:44.810 --> 01:28:49.449
But anyway, I should say I'm not
sure an atheist should really

01:28:49.449 --> 01:28:51.740
talk about this question at
all, so somebody else maybe

01:28:51.740 --> 01:28:52.880
have the--

01:28:52.880 --> 01:28:54.290
LIGHTMAN: Does anyone want to--

01:28:54.290 --> 01:28:55.490
any of you want to--

01:28:55.490 --> 01:28:58.600
SONTAG: I'm afraid, for the
same reason Roger Penrose just

01:28:58.600 --> 01:29:02.255
stated, I would have
to disqualify myself.

01:29:02.255 --> 01:29:02.796
WILSON: Pass.

01:29:08.290 --> 01:29:11.530
LIGHTMAN: Well, we have
run out of time tonight.

01:29:11.530 --> 01:29:13.490
SONTAG: Oh, no.

01:29:13.490 --> 01:29:14.230
A couple more.

01:29:14.230 --> 01:29:15.855
LIGHTMAN: Should we
have a couple more?

01:29:15.855 --> 01:29:17.104
AUDIENCE: Yeah, a couple more.

01:29:20.580 --> 01:29:23.180
LIGHTMAN: We'll have two
more comments or questions

01:29:23.180 --> 01:29:26.050
from the audience
from both sides here.

01:29:26.050 --> 01:29:28.460
AUDIENCE: I'd like to ask Ms.
Sontag about her experience

01:29:28.460 --> 01:29:31.490
last month in accepting the
Jerusalem Prize, in which she

01:29:31.490 --> 01:29:34.440
challenged some of the popular
images and meanings that

01:29:34.440 --> 01:29:37.350
go along with it in
the political arena.

01:29:37.350 --> 01:29:38.850
I believe you were
quoted as saying,

01:29:38.850 --> 01:29:41.630
"I believe the doctrine of
collective responsibility

01:29:41.630 --> 01:29:44.000
as a rationale for
collective punishment

01:29:44.000 --> 01:29:47.000
is never justified
militarily or ethically.

01:29:47.000 --> 01:29:49.370
And I mean, of course,
the disproportionate use

01:29:49.370 --> 01:29:53.940
of firepower against civilians,
the demolition of their homes,

01:29:53.940 --> 01:29:56.360
the destruction of their
orchards and groves,

01:29:56.360 --> 01:29:58.250
the deprivation of
their livelihood

01:29:58.250 --> 01:30:02.060
and access to employment, to
schooling, medical services,

01:30:02.060 --> 01:30:04.670
or as a punishment for
hostile military activities

01:30:04.670 --> 01:30:06.270
in the vicinity of
those civilians."

01:30:06.270 --> 01:30:08.925
And you are, of course,
referring to the Palestinians.

01:30:08.925 --> 01:30:10.610
And I congratulate
you for making

01:30:10.610 --> 01:30:13.250
that statement, more or less
in the jaws of the lion.

01:30:13.250 --> 01:30:15.650
And I wonder what sort of
reaction you've gotten for it.

01:30:15.650 --> 01:30:17.870
And what sort of
visual images would

01:30:17.870 --> 01:30:20.900
make those sentiments come
home to people in America that

01:30:20.900 --> 01:30:23.650
paid a lot of the bills for
those types of activities

01:30:23.650 --> 01:30:28.280
and to people in
Jerusalem where you spoke?

01:30:28.280 --> 01:30:30.187
[APPLAUSE]

01:30:33.923 --> 01:30:38.260
SONTAG: I only like to
make political statements

01:30:38.260 --> 01:30:40.860
about things that
I know firsthand.

01:30:40.860 --> 01:30:42.350
I don't think it's appropriate.

01:30:42.350 --> 01:30:44.370
I mean, I thank you
for your support.

01:30:44.370 --> 01:30:48.520
But I think we should stay
with the subject this evening.

01:30:48.520 --> 01:30:51.840
Maybe we can talk
about it after.

01:30:51.840 --> 01:30:54.390
I do stand very much
for those sentiments.

01:30:54.390 --> 01:30:56.020
I was very glad to
make that statement,

01:30:56.020 --> 01:30:57.800
and I feel very
passionately about it,

01:30:57.800 --> 01:30:59.190
but it's not the
subject tonight.

01:30:59.190 --> 01:31:00.870
LIGHTMAN: One more
question or comment.

01:31:00.870 --> 01:31:01.630
AUDIENCE: Hi.

01:31:01.630 --> 01:31:05.464
This isn't for Ms. Sontag,
nor is it about images.

01:31:05.464 --> 01:31:06.880
This is a question
for Dr. Wilson.

01:31:09.580 --> 01:31:11.830
I personally have
always found myself kind

01:31:11.830 --> 01:31:14.560
of not really knowing
what I want to do,

01:31:14.560 --> 01:31:18.610
drifting around, without
really a sense of purpose.

01:31:18.610 --> 01:31:24.070
But you've taken ants, and
you've made them your life.

01:31:24.070 --> 01:31:27.520
And I was wondering
how you can--

01:31:27.520 --> 01:31:30.970
I could never imagine
taking something so specific

01:31:30.970 --> 01:31:33.940
and indulging myself
completely in it.

01:31:33.940 --> 01:31:35.650
And I was wondering whether--

01:31:35.650 --> 01:31:39.360
I mean, how you
think your life is--

01:31:39.360 --> 01:31:42.947
how, by focusing on something so
specifically, you think that--

01:31:42.947 --> 01:31:44.530
do you think that
you have a broader--

01:31:48.769 --> 01:31:49.310
I don't know.

01:31:49.310 --> 01:31:53.450
Just your thoughts
on it, really.

01:31:53.450 --> 01:31:56.390
[APPLAUSE]

01:32:03.760 --> 01:32:07.960
WILSON: I think the greatest
advice, possibly the most

01:32:07.960 --> 01:32:10.720
effective advice, I've ever
given to young scientists

01:32:10.720 --> 01:32:15.420
is to find the subject in
the great temple of science--

01:32:15.420 --> 01:32:18.630
it's the room and the
bench and the subject

01:32:18.630 --> 01:32:25.017
that you feel a surge of passion
of interest in, and go for it.

01:32:25.017 --> 01:32:26.100
That's the best I can say.

01:32:26.100 --> 01:32:29.988
AUDIENCE: How did you become
passionate about ants?

01:32:29.988 --> 01:32:33.390
SONTAG: Let somebody--
let's let in a higher--

01:32:33.390 --> 01:32:35.640
WILSON: Why did I become
passionate about ants?

01:32:35.640 --> 01:32:36.567
AUDIENCE: Yes.

01:32:36.567 --> 01:32:38.400
SONTAG: Most interesting
thing in the world.

01:32:38.400 --> 01:32:39.400
WILSON: Let me suggest--

01:32:41.892 --> 01:32:42.740
SONTAG: Go for it.

01:32:42.740 --> 01:32:43.240
Go for it.

01:32:43.240 --> 01:32:46.160
WILSON: --at the
next opportunity,

01:32:46.160 --> 01:32:49.000
you put down some
cookie crumbles

01:32:49.000 --> 01:32:53.230
on the ground near an ant
nest and watch and start

01:32:53.230 --> 01:32:54.240
studying them.

01:32:54.240 --> 01:32:59.320
And you will see what comes
as close to what life must

01:32:59.320 --> 01:33:03.220
be like, might be like, on
another planet as you might

01:33:03.220 --> 01:33:06.552
hope ever to see on this one.

01:33:06.552 --> 01:33:09.450
[APPLAUSE]

01:33:14.402 --> 01:33:16.360
LIGHTMAN: I'm going to
allow one more question,

01:33:16.360 --> 01:33:18.160
and then we have to end.

01:33:18.160 --> 01:33:19.480
AUDIENCE: I'd like to ask it.

01:33:19.480 --> 01:33:21.340
It's poignant.

01:33:21.340 --> 01:33:23.650
LIGHTMAN: The question
is coming from over here.

01:33:23.650 --> 01:33:26.430
Can you ask a comment
or question, please?

01:33:26.430 --> 01:33:27.859
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

01:33:27.859 --> 01:33:29.650
I was wondering if the
members of the panel

01:33:29.650 --> 01:33:34.120
could comment about the
importance of images

01:33:34.120 --> 01:33:38.590
for the education of
the general public

01:33:38.590 --> 01:33:42.130
about science, including
the notion that perhaps

01:33:42.130 --> 01:33:45.400
images could contribute to
the inspiration of the public

01:33:45.400 --> 01:33:48.170
toward supporting great
scientific achievements,

01:33:48.170 --> 01:33:50.140
such as a trip to Mars?

01:33:50.140 --> 01:33:52.330
Or simply the
importance of images

01:33:52.330 --> 01:33:55.890
that serve to draw the public
into learning about the science

01:33:55.890 --> 01:33:57.770
about the world around us?

01:34:01.060 --> 01:34:04.780
WILSON: Alan, excuse me,
why don't you answer that?

01:34:04.780 --> 01:34:05.790
SONTAG: Yes.

01:34:05.790 --> 01:34:08.180
WILSON: We've left
Alan out of this a bit,

01:34:08.180 --> 01:34:11.370
and Alan is one of our most
distinguished and practiced

01:34:11.370 --> 01:34:14.850
expositors of just the
subject of that question.

01:34:17.736 --> 01:34:20.622
[LAUGHTER]

01:34:20.622 --> 01:34:23.040
This is known as
Wilson's revenge.

01:34:23.040 --> 01:34:32.030
LIGHTMAN: Well, I think
that images inspire us,

01:34:32.030 --> 01:34:35.760
just as novels do.

01:34:35.760 --> 01:34:41.610
And you're really speaking
about looking to the future

01:34:41.610 --> 01:34:47.610
and getting excitement
for the future.

01:34:47.610 --> 01:34:56.300
And I believe that a
picture of Mars from space,

01:34:56.300 --> 01:35:01.210
the Mars pictures, whatever
the scientific enterprise is--

01:35:01.210 --> 01:35:04.870
a picture of Ed Wilson's ants--

01:35:04.870 --> 01:35:07.950
I think inspire us.

01:35:07.950 --> 01:35:10.510
I know that's a trite
phrase, but it's true.

01:35:10.510 --> 01:35:13.300
I mean, we've all
reacted to these images

01:35:13.300 --> 01:35:16.420
in a very visceral way.

01:35:16.420 --> 01:35:22.920
Susan pointed out that
those are somewhat--

01:35:22.920 --> 01:35:26.440
they're all pictures
that we've seen before.

01:35:26.440 --> 01:35:29.380
And yet, they still
have the power

01:35:29.380 --> 01:35:31.930
to evoke a reaction to us.

01:35:31.930 --> 01:35:34.450
As Roger Penrose
began speaking, we

01:35:34.450 --> 01:35:38.140
could see that he
was emotionally

01:35:38.140 --> 01:35:40.150
affected by these images.

01:35:40.150 --> 01:35:43.840
And I think that
images have that power.

01:35:43.840 --> 01:35:48.640
And to the extent that the
scientific enterprise is

01:35:48.640 --> 01:35:56.660
a human enterprise, that our
emotional support is important,

01:35:56.660 --> 01:35:58.900
and images have that power.

01:35:58.900 --> 01:36:00.290
So I will end there.

01:36:00.290 --> 01:36:04.110
And let me thank everyone
for coming tonight.

01:36:04.110 --> 01:36:07.160
[APPLAUSE]

